"type\": \"random\"}, \"in_proj_bias\": {\"type\": \"random\"}, \"conv_weight\": {\"type\": \"random\"}, \"conv_bias\": {\"type\": \"random\"}, \"out_proj_weight\": {\"type\": \"random\"}, \"out_proj_bias\": {\"type\": \"random\"}}, \"tolerance\": {\"max_atol\": 0.021, \"max_rtol\": 0.05}}\n{\"uuid\": \"6033fd0f-a2c3-5f50-89e8-ac4aa0274a0e\", \"axes\": {\"batch_size\": 2, \"seq_len\": 4096}, \"inputs\": {\"x\": {\"type\": \"random\"}, \"in_proj_weight\": {\"type\": \"random\"}, \"in_proj_bias\": {\"type\": \"random\"}, \"conv_weight\": {\"type\": \"random\"}, \"conv_bias\": {\"type\": \"random\"}, \"out_proj_weight\": {\"type\": \"random\"}, \"out_proj_bias\": {\"type\": \"random\"}}, \"tolerance\": {\"max_atol\": 0.021, \"max_rtol\": 0.05}}\n{\"uuid\": \"bb262a64-b4a4-5ef1-b5c5-ad69550e822c\", \"axes\": {\"batch_size\": 1, \"seq_len\": 2048}, \"inputs\": {\"x\": {\"type\": \"random\"}, \"in_proj_weight\": {\"type\": \"random\"}, \"in_proj_bias\": {\"type\": \"random\"}, \"conv_weight\": {\"type\": \"random\"}, \"conv_bias\": {\"type\": \"random\"}, \"out_proj_weight\": {\"type\": \"random\"}, \"out_proj_bias\": {\"type\": \"random\"}}, \"tolerance\": {\"max_atol\": 0.009800000000000001, \"max_rtol\": 0.05}}\n","numLines":6,"startLine":1,"totalLines":6}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0147jwqbRyLYMXmP15JKCFri","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t{\n2\t    \"name\": \"005_conv_gated_projection_with_causal_conv\",\n3\t    \"hf_id\": \"LiquidAI/LFM2-1.2B\",\n4\t    \"description\": \"Complete fused convolution layer operation that combines triple linear projection, element-wise gating, causal 1D grouped convolution, gating with output channel, and final output projection. This is the dominant operation in non-attention layers in LFM2 architecture.\",\n5\t    \"axes\": {\n6\t        \"batch_size\": {\n7\t            \"type\": \"var\",\n8\t            \"description\": \"Batch size\"\n9\t        },\n10\t        \"seq_len\": {\n11\t            \"type\": \"var\",\n12\t            \"description\": \"Sequence length\"\n13\t        },\n14\t        \"hidden_size\": {\n15\t            \"type\": \"const\",\n16\t            \"value\": 2048,\n17\t            \"description\": \"Hidden dimension size\"\n18\t        },\n19\t        \"conv_kernel_size\": {\n20\t            \"type\": \"const\",\n21\t            \"value\": 4,\n22\t            \"description\": \"Causal convolution kernel size\"\n23\t        },\n24\t        \"triple_hidden\": {\n25\t            \"type\": \"expr\",\n26\t            \"expression\": \"hidden_size * 3\",\n27\t            \"description\": \"Triple projection output dimension\"\n28\t        }\n29\t    },\n30\t    \"custom_inputs_entrypoint\": null,\n31\t    \"inputs\": {\n32\t        \"x\": {\n33\t            \"shape\": [\n34\t                \"batch_size\",\n35\t                \"seq_len\",\n36\t                \"hidden_size\"\n37\t            ],\n38\t            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n39\t            \"description\": \"Input hidden states\"\n40\t        },\n41\t        \"in_proj_weight\": {\n42\t            \"shape\": [\n43\t                \"triple_hidden\",\n44\t                \"hidden_size\"\n45\t            ],\n46\t            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n47\t            \"description\": \"Input projection weight matrix\"\n48\t        },\n49\t        \"in_proj_bias\": {\n50\t            \"shape\": [\n51\t                \"triple_hidden\"\n52\t            ],\n53\t            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n54\t            \"description\": \"Input projection bias\"\n55\t        },\n56\t        \"conv_weight\": {\n57\t            \"shape\": [\n58\t                \"hidden_size\",\n59\t                \"1\",\n60\t                \"conv_kernel_size\"\n61\t            ],\n62\t            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n63\t            \"description\": \"Grouped conv1d weight (depthwise)\"\n64\t        },\n65\t        \"conv_bias\": {\n66\t            \"shape\": [\n67\t                \"hidden_size\"\n68\t            ],\n69\t            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n70\t            \"description\": \"Grouped conv1d bias\"\n71\t        },\n72\t        \"out_proj_weight\": {\n73\t            \"shape\": [\n74\t                \"hidden_size\",\n75\t                \"hidden_size\"\n76\t            ],\n77\t            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n78\t            \"description\": \"Output projection weight matrix\"\n79\t        },\n80\t        \"out_proj_bias\": {\n81\t            \"shape\": [\n82\t                \"hidden_size\"\n83\t            ],\n84\t            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n85\t            \"description\": \"Output projection bias\"\n86\t        }\n87\t    },\n88\t    \"outputs\": {\n89\t        \"output\": {\n90\t            \"shape\": [\n91\t                \"batch_size\",\n92\t                \"seq_len\",\n93\t                \"hidden_size\"\n94\t            ],\n95\t            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n96\t            \"description\": \"Output hidden states\"\n97\t        }\n98\t    },\n99\t    \"reference\": \"import torch\\nimport torch.nn.functional as F\\n\\n@torch.no_grad()\\ndef run(\\n    x: torch.Tensor,\\n    in_proj_weight: torch.Tensor,\\n    in_proj_bias: torch.Tensor,\\n    conv_weight: torch.Tensor,\\n    conv_bias: torch.Tensor,\\n    out_proj_weight: torch.Tensor,\\n    out_proj_bias: torch.Tensor,\\n):\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    Complete fused convolution layer with gated projections and causal convolution.\\n    \\n    Operation flow:\\n    1. Triple linear projection: x -> (B, C, x_proj) via in_proj\\n    2. Element-wise gating: Bx = B * x_proj\\n    3. Grouped causal 1D convolution on Bx with kernel_size=4\\n    4. Output gating: y = C * conv_out\\n    5. Final output projection: y -> out_proj(y)\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    batch_size, seq_len, hidden_size = x.shape\\n    conv_kernel_size = conv_weight.shape[2]\\n    \\n    # Step 1: Triple linear projection\\n    # Shape: (batch_size, seq_len, 3 * hidden_size)\\n    BCx = F.linear(x, in_proj_weight, in_proj_bias)\\n    \\n    # Transpose for conv1d: (batch_size, 3 * hidden_size, seq_len)\\n    BCx = BCx.transpose(-1, -2)\\n    \\n    # Split into B, C, x_proj along channel dimension\\n    # Each has shape: (batch_size, hidden_size, seq_len)\\n    B, C, x_proj = BCx.chunk(3, dim=1)\\n    \\n    # Step 2: Element-wise gating\\n    # Shape: (batch_size, hidden_size, seq_len)\\n    Bx = B * x_proj\\n    \\n    # Step 3: Grouped causal 1D convolution\\n    # Apply conv with causal padding\\n    # Padding is kernel_size - 1 on the left for causal\\n    Bx_padded = F.pad(Bx, (conv_kernel_size - 1, 0))\\n    \\n    # Grouped conv1d with groups=hidden_size (depthwise)\\n    conv_out = F.conv1d(Bx_padded, conv_weight, conv_bias, groups=hidden_size)\\n    \\n    # Shape: (batch_size, hidden_size, seq_len)\\n    \\n    # Step 4: Output gating with C\\n    # Shape: (batch_size, hidden_size, seq_len)\\n    y = C * conv_out\\n    \\n    # Transpose back: (batch_size, seq_len, hidden_size)\\n    y = y.transpose(-1, -2).contiguous()\\n    \\n    # Step 5: Final output projection\\n    # Shape: (batch_size, seq_len, hidden_size)\\n    output = F.linear(y, out_proj_weight, out_proj_bias)\\n    \\n    return output\\n\"\n100\t}\n101\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a59e6f9d-618a-44bc-8206-5f4b96ad66c3","uuid":"1c240b56-ced2-49f2-88ce-977add5496af","timestamp":"2026-09-16T17:00:50.169Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/data1/workspace/weihongren/kda-runs/formal-kda-20260916--sol_execbench--L1-005_conv_gated_projection_with_causal_conv/task/definition.json","content":"{\n    \"name\": \"005_conv_gated_projection_with_causal_conv\",\n    \"hf_id\": \"LiquidAI/LFM2-1.2B\",\n    \"description\": \"Complete fused convolution layer operation that combines triple linear projection, element-wise gating, causal 1D grouped convolution, gating with output channel, and final output projection. This is the dominant operation in non-attention layers in LFM2 architecture.\",\n    \"axes\": {\n        \"batch_size\": {\n            \"type\": \"var\",\n            \"description\": \"Batch size\"\n        },\n        \"seq_len\": {\n            \"type\": \"var\",\n            \"description\": \"Sequence length\"\n        },\n        \"hidden_size\": {\n            \"type\": \"const\",\n            \"value\": 2048,\n            \"description\": \"Hidden dimension size\"\n        },\n        \"conv_kernel_size\": {\n            \"type\": \"const\",\n            \"value\": 4,\n            \"description\": \"Causal convolution kernel size\"\n        },\n        \"triple_hidden\": {\n            \"type\": \"expr\",\n            \"expression\": \"hidden_size * 3\",\n            \"description\": \"Triple projection output dimension\"\n        }\n    },\n    \"custom_inputs_entrypoint\": null,\n    \"inputs\": {\n        \"x\": {\n            \"shape\": [\n                \"batch_size\",\n                \"seq_len\",\n                \"hidden_size\"\n            ],\n            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n            \"description\": \"Input hidden states\"\n        },\n        \"in_proj_weight\": {\n            \"shape\": [\n                \"triple_hidden\",\n                \"hidden_size\"\n            ],\n            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n            \"description\": \"Input projection weight matrix\"\n        },\n        \"in_proj_bias\": {\n            \"shape\": [\n                \"triple_hidden\"\n            ],\n            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n            \"description\": \"Input projection bias\"\n        },\n        \"conv_weight\": {\n            \"shape\": [\n                \"hidden_size\",\n                \"1\",\n                \"conv_kernel_size\"\n            ],\n            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n            \"description\": \"Grouped conv1d weight (depthwise)\"\n        },\n        \"conv_bias\": {\n            \"shape\": [\n                \"hidden_size\"\n            ],\n            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n            \"description\": \"Grouped conv1d bias\"\n        },\n        \"out_proj_weight\": {\n            \"shape\": [\n                \"hidden_size\",\n                \"hidden_size\"\n            ],\n            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n            \"description\": \"Output projection weight matrix\"\n        },\n        \"out_proj_bias\": {\n            \"shape\": [\n                \"hidden_size\"\n            ],\n            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n            \"description\": \"Output projection bias\"\n        }\n    },\n    \"outputs\": {\n        \"output\": {\n            \"shape\": [\n                \"batch_size\",\n                \"seq_len\",\n                \"hidden_size\"\n            ],\n            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n            \"description\": \"Output hidden states\"\n        }\n    },\n    \"reference\": \"import torch\\nimport torch.nn.functional as F\\n\\n@torch.no_grad()\\ndef run(\\n    x: torch.Tensor,\\n    in_proj_weight: torch.Tensor,\\n    in_proj_bias: torch.Tensor,\\n    conv_weight: torch.Tensor,\\n    conv_bias: torch.Tensor,\\n    out_proj_weight: torch.Tensor,\\n    out_proj_bias: torch.Tensor,\\n):\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    Complete fused convolution layer with gated projections and causal convolution.\\n    \\n    Operation flow:\\n    1. Triple linear projection: x -> (B, C, x_proj) via in_proj\\n    2. Element-wise gating: Bx = B * x_proj\\n    3. Grouped causal 1D convolution on Bx with kernel_size=4\\n    4. Output gating: y = C * conv_out\\n    5. Final output projection: y -> out_proj(y)\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    batch_size, seq_len, hidden_size = x.shape\\n    conv_kernel_size = conv_weight.shape[2]\\n    \\n    # Step 1: Triple linear projection\\n    # Shape: (batch_size, seq_len, 3 * hidden_size)\\n    BCx = F.linear(x, in_proj_weight, in_proj_bias)\\n    \\n    # Transpose for conv1d: (batch_size, 3 * hidden_size, seq_len)\\n    BCx = BCx.transpose(-1, -2)\\n    \\n    # Split into B, C, x_proj along channel dimension\\n    # Each has shape: (batch_size, hidden_size, seq_len)\\n    B, C, x_proj = BCx.chunk(3, dim=1)\\n    \\n    # Step 2: Element-wise gating\\n    # Shape: (batch_size, hidden_size, seq_len)\\n    Bx = B * x_proj\\n    \\n    # Step 3: Grouped causal 1D convolution\\n    # Apply conv with causal padding\\n    # Padding is kernel_size - 1 on the left for causal\\n    Bx_padded = F.pad(Bx, (conv_kernel_size - 1, 0))\\n    \\n    # Grouped conv1d with groups=hidden_size (depthwise)\\n    conv_out = F.conv1d(Bx_padded, conv_weight, conv_bias, groups=hidden_size)\\n    \\n    # Shape: (batch_size, hidden_size, seq_len)\\n    \\n    # Step 4: Output gating with C\\n    # Shape: (batch_size, hidden_size, seq_len)\\n    y = C * conv_out\\n    \\n    # Transpose back: (batch_size, seq_len, hidden_size)\\n    y = y.transpose(-1, -2).contiguous()\\n    \\n    # Step 5: Final output projection\\n    # Shape: (batch_size, seq_len, hidden_size)\\n    output = F.linear(y, out_proj_weight, out_proj_bias)\\n    \\n    return output\\n\"\n}\n","numLines":101,"startLine":1,"totalLines":101}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JzQUtNDfibPbaKeSt1DSrC","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Draft — L1/005 `conv_gated_projection_with_causal_conv`\n2\t\n3\tTarget HW: NVIDIA **A800 (`sm_80`, Ampere)**. Primary impl **must be Triton**; PyTorch only for\n4\tmetadata/launch plumbing. No Torch/CPU/NumPy/CUDA-ext computational fallback. Metric: **geomean\n5\tspeedup vs the reference across the 5 fixed feedback workloads, with every selected workload passing\n6\tcorrectness**. Budget: 100 candidate evals; token soft 1.0M / hard 1.2M.\n7\t\n8\t---\n9\t\n10\t## 1. What the operation computes\n11\t\n12\tSignature (all tensors `bfloat16`), constants `H = hidden_size = 2048`, `K_c = conv_kernel_size = 4`,\n13\t`3H = triple_hidden = 6144`:\n14\t\n15\t```\n16\tx               : (B, S, H)\n17\tin_proj_weight  : (3H, H)      in_proj_bias : (3H,)\n18\tconv_weight     : (H, 1, K_c)  conv_bias    : (H,)      # depthwise, groups = H\n19\tout_proj_weight : (H, H)       out_proj_bias: (H,)\n20\t-> output       : (B, S, H)\n21\t```\n22\t\n23\tReference flow (from `task/definition.json`):\n24\t\n25\t1. `BCx = F.linear(x, in_proj_weight, in_proj_bias)` → `(B, S, 3H)`  *(GEMM1)*\n26\t2. transpose to `(B, 3H, S)`, `chunk(3, dim=1)` → `Bgate, Cgate, Xproj`, each `(B, H, S)`\n27\t3. `Bx = Bgate * Xproj`  *(element-wise gating)*\n28\t4. `Bx_padded = F.pad(Bx, (K_c-1, 0))` then `F.conv1d(..., groups=H)` → `conv_out (B,H,S)` *(depthwise causal conv)*\n29\t5. `y = Cgate * conv_out`  *(output gating)*\n30\t6. transpose back to `(B, S, H)`, `.contiguous()`\n31\t7. `output = F.linear(y, out_proj_weight, out_proj_bias)` *(GEMM2)*\n32\t\n33\t### 1.1 Exact index mapping (critical for correctness)\n34\t\n35\tFlatten rows as `m = b*S + s`, with `M = B*S`, `m ∈ [0,M)`, `s = m mod S`, `b = m // S`.\n36\t\n37\tAfter the transpose+chunk, for output channel `h ∈ [0,H)`:\n38\t- `Bgate[b,h,s] = BCx[b,s, 0*H + h]`\n39\t- `Cgate[b,h,s] = BCx[b,s, 1*H + h]`\n40\t- `Xproj[b,h,s] = BCx[b,s, 2*H + h]`\n41\t\n42\tSo the three chunks are **contiguous column blocks** of `BCx`: `[0,H)`, `[H,2H)`, `[2H,3H)`.\n43\t\n44\tGating: `Bx[m,h] = BCx[m,h] * BCx[m, 2H+h]`.\n45\t\n46\tCausal depthwise conv (`F.pad` left = `K_c-1 = 3`, no right pad, stride 1, kernel 4). For tap\n47\t`k ∈ {0,1,2,3}` the padded input aligns so that output position `s` reads `Bx` at position `s-3+k`:\n48\t\n49\t```\n50\tconv_out[m,h] = conv_bias[h] + Σ_{k=0..3} conv_weight[h,0,k] * Bx[b, h, s-3+k]\n51\t              where Bx[b,h,·] taps at s-3+k are ZERO when (s-3+k) < 0   (causal left pad)\n52\t```\n53\t\n54\t- Tap `k=3` = **current** position `s` (weight `conv_weight[h,0,3]`).\n55\t- Tap `k=0` = oldest position `s-3` (weight `conv_weight[h,0,0]`).\n56\t- In flattened rows, position `s-3+k` is row `m-3+k`, valid only while it stays **in the same batch**\n57\t  (`s-3+k ≥ 0`). Rows crossing below `s=0` are the causal zero pad → must be masked to 0. Because rows\n58\t  are laid out `b*S + s`, staying `≥ b*S` is exactly the `s-3+k ≥ 0` condition, so the batch boundary\n59\t  and the causal pad coincide — a single `(s-3+k) ≥ 0` guard handles both.\n60\t\n61\tOutput gating: `y[m,h] = BCx[m, H+h] * conv_out[m,h]`.\n62\t\n63\tGEMM2 (note `F.linear` uses `W^T`): `output[m, n] = out_proj_bias[n] + Σ_{h} y[m,h] * out_proj_weight[n,h]`.\n64\t\n65\tLikewise GEMM1: `BCx[m, j] = in_proj_bias[j] + Σ_{i} x[m,i] * in_proj_weight[j,i]`.\n66\t\n67\t**Layout win:** everything lives in `(M, ·)` row-major space (`x → (M,H)` is a free view of the\n68\t`(B,S,H)` contiguous tensor; `y → (M,H)`). The reference's two `transpose`s and `.contiguous()` copy\n69\tare only artifacts of routing data through `conv1d`'s `(B,C,S)` convention. If we run the conv along\n70\t`M`-rows directly we can **eliminate both transposes and the contiguous copy entirely**.\n71\t\n72\t---\n73\t\n74\t## 2. Workloads and problem sizes\n75\t\n76\t| # | uuid (short) | B  | S    | M=B·S | atol   | rtol |\n77\t|---|--------------|----|------|-------|--------|------|\n78\t| 1 | b0a9e3f0     | 1  | 1024 | 1024  | 0.0130 | 0.05 |\n79\t| 2 | 8b678b13     | 4  | 256  | 1024  | 0.0130 | 0.05 |\n80\t| 3 | 58e3ac47     | 32 | 256  | 8192  | 0.0210 | 0.05 |\n81\t| 4 | 6033fd0f     | 2  | 4096 | 8192  | 0.0210 | 0.05 |\n82\t| 5 | bb262a64     | 1  | 2048 | 2048  | 0.0098 | 0.05 |\n83\t\n84\tObservations:\n85\t- `H=2048`, `3H=6144`, `K_c=4`, `groups=H` are **compile-time constants** — bake them in and\n86\t  specialize kernels / `tl.constexpr`.\n87\t- Effective GEMM shapes: **GEMM1** is `(M×2048) · (2048×6144)`; **GEMM2** is `(M×2048) · (2048×2048)`.\n88\t- `M` takes only **three distinct values** {1024, 2048, 8192}. `S` takes {256,1024,2048,4096}. Small\n89\t  distinct set → autotune configs will generalize well; `M` is always a multiple of 256 and 1024,\n90\t  and `S` is always a power-of-two ≥ 256 (helpful for conv tiling / no ragged masks on `M` within a batch).\n91\t- Per-workload compute (FLOPs): GEMM1 ≈ `2·M·2048·6144`, GEMM2 ≈ `2·M·2048·2048` → **GEMM1 is 3×\n92\t  GEMM2**. The middle (gating+conv+gating) is tiny FLOPs but touches `~5·M·H` bf16 elements → memory-bound.\n93\t- rtol 5% is loose; atol is the tighter constraint (0.0098 on WL5, 0.013 on WL1/2, 0.021 on WL3/4).\n94\t  WL5 (B=1, S=2048, atol 0.0098) is the least forgiving.\n95\t\n96\t---\n97\t\n98\t## 3. Constraints & environment realities\n99\t\n100\t- **Triton-only compute.** Both GEMMs must be Triton `tl.dot` kernels (cannot call `F.linear`/`torch.matmul`\n101\t  for compute — that would be a Torch computational fallback). This is the central performance risk:\n102\t  we must approach cuBLAS bf16 throughput on Ampere with a well-tuned Triton matmul, then win the rest\n103\t  via fusion.\n104\t- **No local execution / no profiler.** Per CLAUDE.md we may not run CUDA, a profiler, `nvidia-smi`,\n105\t  the external evaluator directly, or any alternate correctness harness. The **only** feedback channel\n106\t  is `./scripts/evaluate_candidate.sh feedback cNNN`, and each such run (5 workloads) consumes **one**\n107\t  of 100 evals and reports correctness + speedup. ⇒ correctness must be established **analytically**\n108\t  before spending an eval; a failed Triton run is wasted budget.\n109\t- **Skills:** `KernelWiki` targets Blackwell/Hopper (tcgen05/TMEM/CLC/FP4) and is **not applicable** to\n110\t  `sm_80`; `ncu-report-skill` requires running the profiler, which is disallowed. Both are effectively\n111\t  unusable here beyond generic reading; note in candidate `skill_usage` accordingly.\n112\t- **Immutability:** every meaningful source/config/launch change ⇒ new `cNNN`; never rewrite past records.\n113\t\n114\t---\n115\t\n116\t## 4. Numerical analysis & risks\n117\t\n118\t### 4.1 Reference precision chain\n119\tReference rounds to **bf16 at every stage**: `BCx` (fp32-accum cuBLAS → bf16), `Bx = Bgate*Xproj`\n120\t(bf16 op, internally fp32→round bf16), `conv_out` (cuDNN bf16, fp32-accum → bf16), `y = Cgate*conv_out`\n121\t(→ bf16), then GEMM2 (fp32-accum → bf16). So the \"ground truth\" itself carries bf16 rounding at 4\n122\tintermediate points.\n123\t\n124\t### 4.2 Our precision plan\n125\t- **GEMMs:** load bf16 operands, accumulate in **fp32** (`tl.dot(..., out_dtype=fp32)` / fp32\n126\t  accumulator), add bias in fp32, cast to bf16 on store. Matches cuBLAS behavior.\n127\t- **Middle (gating+conv+gating):** compute entirely in **fp32** (promote loaded bf16 → fp32, multiply,\n128\t  conv-accumulate in fp32, multiply, then cast the final `y` to bf16). This is *more* accurate than the\n129\t  reference's intermediate bf16 roundings, and the differences are well inside the loose tolerances.\n130\t  - Key subtlety: the reference rounds `Bx` to **bf16 before** the conv. If our fp32 path ever drifts\n131\t    outside atol we can insert an explicit `Bx_bf16 = Bx.to(bf16).to(fp32)` round to mirror the\n132\t    reference; keep this as a fallback lever (WL5 with atol 0.0098 is where to watch).\n133\t- **Accumulation order:** conv is only 4 taps → order-insensitive. GEMM2 reduces over `H=2048`; use\n134\t  the standard `BLOCK_K` split with fp32 accumulator (cuBLAS also splits K). Differences here are\n135\t  random-sign and bounded by rtol 0.05 over 2048 terms.\n136\t\n137\t### 4.3 Correctness pitfalls to get right the first time\n138\t1. **Tap order / causal direction:** `k=3` is the current sample; taps go backward in `s`. A mirrored\n139\t   kernel (using `conv_weight[...,K_c-1-k]` or reading `s+k`) will silently pass shape checks but fail\n140\t   numerically. Derive from `F.pad(left=3)` + `conv1d` cross-correlation semantics (PyTorch conv1d is\n141\t   cross-correlation, **not** flipped convolution).\n142\t2. **Batch boundaries:** taps at `s-3+k<0` must be zero; never read row `m-3+k` from the previous batch.\n143\t   Guard with `s - 3 + k ≥ 0` (equivalently `row ≥ b*S`).\n144\t3. **Chunk offsets:** `Bgate=[0,H)`, `Cgate=[H,2H)`, `Xproj=[2H,3H)` — off-by-`H` here is a classic bug.\n145\t4. **`F.linear` transpose:** weights are `(out, in)`; GEMM computes `Σ_in x·W[out,in]` (i.e. `x @ W^T`).\n146\t5. **Bias dtype:** biases are bf16; add in fp32 then round.\n147\t6. **Contiguity of `x`:** reshape `(B,S,H)→(M,H)` is only free if `x` is contiguous (it is per definition);\n148\t   assert strides / use `.reshape` guarded, but do not silently `.contiguous()` an already-contiguous tensor.\n149\t\n150\t---\n151\t\n152\t## 5. Triton design space\n153\t\n154\tBaseline decomposition into **3 Triton kernels** (all in `(M,·)` space, no transposes):\n155\t\n156\t### K1 — GEMM1 with bias: `BCx = x @ in_proj_weight^T + in_proj_bias`, shape `(M, 3H)`\n157\t- Standard tiled bf16 matmul: `BLOCK_M × BLOCK_N` output tile, `BLOCK_K` reduction over `H=2048`,\n158\t  fp32 accumulator, L2-friendly `GROUP_M` row-grouping, `num_warps`/`num_stages` autotuned.\n159\t- `N=6144` and `K=2048` are multiples of 64/128 → no N/K masking; `M∈{1024,2048,8192}` multiples of\n160\t  128 → no M masking for `BLOCK_M∈{64,128,256}`. Clean, mask-free fast path.\n161\t- Candidate tile menu: `BLOCK_M∈{64,128,256}`, `BLOCK_N∈{64,128,256}`, `BLOCK_K∈{32,64,128}`,\n162\t  `num_stages∈{3,4,5}`, `num_warps∈{4,8}`, `GROUP_M∈{4,8}`.\n163\t\n164\t### K2 — fused gating + causal depthwise conv + gating: `y (M, H)`\n165\t- Reads `BCx`, computes `Bx=BCx[:, :H]*BCx[:, 2H:3H]`, the 4-tap causal conv (per-channel weights\n166\t  `conv_weight[h,0,·]`, `conv_bias[h]`), then `y = BCx[:, H:2H] * conv_out`. Pure element-wise + tiny\n167\t  stencil ⇒ **memory-bound**; goal is minimum passes.\n168\t- Tiling options:\n169\t  - (a) **Sequence-tiled**: block `(BLOCK_S rows within one batch) × (BLOCK_H channels)`; load a\n170\t    halo of 3 extra rows above the tile, compute `Bx` once, do the 4-tap stencil in registers/smem.\n171\t    Amortizes the `Bx` computation and the neighbor reads. Grid = `(B, ceil(S/BLOCK_S), ceil(H/BLOCK_H))`.\n172\t  - (b) **Row-parallel** (simple): one program per `(row-block, channel-block)`, recompute the ≤4\n173\t    neighbor `Bx` values by re-loading `Bgate,Xproj` for rows `m-3..m`. Simplest to prove correct;\n174\t    up to 4× redundant loads of `Bgate/Xproj` but conv is cheap. Good **c001** choice.\n175\t  - Batch-boundary guard via `s` index; conv weights broadcast over the row dimension (per-channel).\n176\t- Because `conv_weight`/`conv_bias` are `(H,·)` and reused for all `M` rows, keep them resident per\n177\t  channel-block.\n178\t\n179\t### K3 — GEMM2 with bias: `output = y @ out_proj_weight^T + out_proj_bias`, shape `(M, H)`\n180\t- Same matmul template as K1 with `N=2048`.\n181\t\n182\t### Fusion escalation ladder (later candidates)\n183\t- **F1: fold gating into GEMM1 epilogue.** Produce only `Bx (M,H)` and `Cgate (M,H)` (write `2H`\n184\t  instead of `3H`) by combining the `[0,H)` and `[2H,3H)` column tiles in the epilogue. Needs the\n185\t  epilogue to see both column blocks → either `BLOCK_N` spanning matched columns or a two-pass\n186\t  accumulator; evaluate cost/benefit (saves ~1/3 of the intermediate write+read).\n187\t- **F2: fuse conv into GEMM2's A-load.** GEMM2's `A = y (M,H)`; `y[m,h]` needs `conv_out[m,h]` which\n188\t  needs neighbor rows `m-1..m-3`. Feasible only if `BLOCK_M` rows are contiguous in `M` and we load a\n189\t  3-row halo into the K-loop; complex and risks correctness at batch/tile edges. Treat as a stretch goal\n190\t  only if K2 is shown to be a real bottleneck.\n191\t- **F3: merge K1+K2** so the gating/conv is the GEMM1 epilogue producing `y` directly. Blocked by the\n192\t  conv needing cross-row (cross-`BLOCK_M`) neighbors; likely not worth it. Keep K2 standalone.\n193\t- **GEMM1 restructuring:** single `N=3H` GEMM reuses each `A` tile across all three projections (best A\n194\t  reuse) — prefer over three separate GEMMs.\n195\t\n196\t### Where the time goes (rough Ampere model, M=8192 case)\n197\t- GEMM1 ≈ `2·8192·2048·6144 ≈ 2.06e11` FLOP; GEMM2 ≈ `6.9e10` FLOP; total ≈ `2.75e11`.\n198\t- At ~1.5e14 bf16 FLOP/s realized → ~1.8 ms of unavoidable matmul; K2 memory traffic\n199\t  (`~5·M·H·2 B ≈ 0.17 GB`) at ~1.5 TB/s → ~0.1 ms. **GEMMs dominate.**\n200\t- ⇒ The realistic upside is: (i) our Triton GEMMs must land near cuBLAS, and (ii) we recover the\n201\t  reference's overheads — the slow **cuDNN depthwise `conv1d` with groups=2048**, two transposes, a\n202\t  `.contiguous()` copy, and several extra kernel launches / intermediate allocations. Grouped/depthwise\n203\t  conv over 2048 groups is a known cuDNN weak spot; that plus the transposes is our headroom.\n204\t- **Speedup expectation:** modest, roughly **1.1–1.5×** if GEMMs match cuBLAS; **<1×** risk if the\n205\t  Triton GEMMs underperform cuBLAS by more than the fusion savings. This risk ordering drives the\n206\t  candidate roadmap: first prove correctness + measure the GEMM gap, then tune GEMMs, then tighten fusion.\n207\t\n208\t---\n209\t\n210\t## 6. Validation strategy (given no local execution)\n211\t\n212\t1. **Analytical correctness before any eval.** Re-derive every index map (§1.1, §4.3) and shape on\n213\t   paper; write kernels with `constexpr` H/3H/K_c so shapes are self-documenting. Add cheap host-side\n214\t   `assert`s on dtypes/shapes/contiguity in `run(...)` (metadata only — allowed).\n215\t2. **Start simple (c001 = correctness anchor):** 3 straightforward kernels (K1, K2 row-parallel variant\n216\t   b, K3), conservative single tile config, fp32 middle. The goal of c001 is a **passing, well-defined\n217\t   baseline** and a first speedup number, not peak performance.\n218\t3. **One variable per candidate.** After c001 passes, change exactly one thing per new `cNNN`\n219\t   (autotune GEMM1, then GEMM2, then K2 sequence-tiling, then F1 fusion), so each eval attributes its\n220\t   delta cleanly. Record parent/hash/hypothesis/per-workload result/geomean/decision/eval-count.\n221\t4. **Correctness watch-list:** WL5 (atol 0.0098) is the tightest — if a candidate fails only there,\n222\t   suspect the fp32-vs-bf16 `Bx` rounding (§4.2) or a conv tap/edge bug, not the GEMMs.\n223\t5. **Budget discipline:** 100 evals but token budget is the likely binding constraint; avoid\n224\t   speculative evals. Converge when geomean gains stall, then write `SEARCH_COMPLETE`. Never run `final`\n225\t   without operator approval.\n226\t\n227\t---\n228\t\n229\t## 7. Open questions to resolve empirically (via feedback evals)\n230\t- How close does a tuned Triton bf16 matmul get to cuBLAS on A800 for these `M,N,K`? (Sets the ceiling.)\n231\t- How large is the reference's `conv1d(groups=2048)`+transpose+contiguous overhead as a fraction of\n232\t  total? (Sets our headroom.) Inferred only from the end-to-end speedup deltas across candidates.\n233\t- Is K2 better as sequence-tiled (halo reuse) or row-parallel (simple)? Decide after c001’s number.\n234\t- Does F1 (gating-in-epilogue) pay for its added complexity, or is the standalone memory-bound K2\n235\t  already negligible vs the GEMMs?\n236\t\n237\t## 8. Immediate next step\n238\tWrite `docs/plan.md` with the executable candidate roadmap (c001 correctness anchor → GEMM autotune →\n239\tK2 tiling → fusion escalation), then implement `c001`.\n240\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a59e6f9d-618a-44bc-8206-5f4b96ad66c3","uuid":"56d12740-57ad-4c6c-91e1-dc012356d0be","timestamp":"2026-09-16T17:00:50.170Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/data1/workspace/weihongren/kda-runs/formal-kda-20260916--sol_execbench--L1-005_conv_gated_projection_with_causal_conv/docs/draft.md","content":"# Draft — L1/005 `conv_gated_projection_with_causal_conv`\n\nTarget HW: NVIDIA **A800 (`sm_80`, Ampere)**. Primary impl **must be Triton**; PyTorch only for\nmetadata/launch plumbing. No Torch/CPU/NumPy/CUDA-ext computational fallback. Metric: **geomean\nspeedup vs the reference across the 5 fixed feedback workloads, with every selected workload passing\ncorrectness**. Budget: 100 candidate evals; token soft 1.0M / hard 1.2M.\n\n---\n\n## 1. What the operation computes\n\nSignature (all tensors `bfloat16`), constants `H = hidden_size = 2048`, `K_c = conv_kernel_size = 4`,\n`3H = triple_hidden = 6144`:\n\n```\nx               : (B, S, H)\nin_proj_weight  : (3H, H)      in_proj_bias : (3H,)\nconv_weight     : (H, 1, K_c)  conv_bias    : (H,)      # depthwise, groups = H\nout_proj_weight : (H, H)       out_proj_bias: (H,)\n-> output       : (B, S, H)\n```\n\nReference flow (from `task/definition.json`):\n\n1. `BCx = F.linear(x, in_proj_weight, in_proj_bias)` → `(B, S, 3H)`  *(GEMM1)*\n2. transpose to `(B, 3H, S)`, `chunk(3, dim=1)` → `Bgate, Cgate, Xproj`, each `(B, H, S)`\n3. `Bx = Bgate * Xproj`  *(element-wise gating)*\n4. `Bx_padded = F.pad(Bx, (K_c-1, 0))` then `F.conv1d(..., groups=H)` → `conv_out (B,H,S)` *(depthwise causal conv)*\n5. `y = Cgate * conv_out`  *(output gating)*\n6. transpose back to `(B, S, H)`, `.contiguous()`\n7. `output = F.linear(y, out_proj_weight, out_proj_bias)` *(GEMM2)*\n\n### 1.1 Exact index mapping (critical for correctness)\n\nFlatten rows as `m = b*S + s`, with `M = B*S`, `m ∈ [0,M)`, `s = m mod S`, `b = m // S`.\n\nAfter the transpose+chunk, for output channel `h ∈ [0,H)`:\n- `Bgate[b,h,s] = BCx[b,s, 0*H + h]`\n- `Cgate[b,h,s] = BCx[b,s, 1*H + h]`\n- `Xproj[b,h,s] = BCx[b,s, 2*H + h]`\n\nSo the three chunks are **contiguous column blocks** of `BCx`: `[0,H)`, `[H,2H)`, `[2H,3H)`.\n\nGating: `Bx[m,h] = BCx[m,h] * BCx[m, 2H+h]`.\n\nCausal depthwise conv (`F.pad` left = `K_c-1 = 3`, no right pad, stride 1, kernel 4). For tap\n`k ∈ {0,1,2,3}` the padded input aligns so that output position `s` reads `Bx` at position `s-3+k`:\n\n```\nconv_out[m,h] = conv_bias[h] + Σ_{k=0..3} conv_weight[h,0,k] * Bx[b, h, s-3+k]\n              where Bx[b,h,·] taps at s-3+k are ZERO when (s-3+k) < 0   (causal left pad)\n```\n\n- Tap `k=3` = **current** position `s` (weight `conv_weight[h,0,3]`).\n- Tap `k=0` = oldest position `s-3` (weight `conv_weight[h,0,0]`).\n- In flattened rows, position `s-3+k` is row `m-3+k`, valid only while it stays **in the same batch**\n  (`s-3+k ≥ 0`). Rows crossing below `s=0` are the causal zero pad → must be masked to 0. Because rows\n  are laid out `b*S + s`, staying `≥ b*S` is exactly the `s-3+k ≥ 0` condition, so the batch boundary\n  and the causal pad coincide — a single `(s-3+k) ≥ 0` guard handles both.\n\nOutput gating: `y[m,h] = BCx[m, H+h] * conv_out[m,h]`.\n\nGEMM2 (note `F.linear` uses `W^T`): `output[m, n] = out_proj_bias[n] + Σ_{h} y[m,h] * out_proj_weight[n,h]`.\n\nLikewise GEMM1: `BCx[m, j] = in_proj_bias[j] + Σ_{i} x[m,i] * in_proj_weight[j,i]`.\n\n**Layout win:** everything lives in `(M, ·)` row-major space (`x → (M,H)` is a free view of the\n`(B,S,H)` contiguous tensor; `y → (M,H)`). The reference's two `transpose`s and `.contiguous()` copy\nare only artifacts of routing data through `conv1d`'s `(B,C,S)` convention. If we run the conv along\n`M`-rows directly we can **eliminate both transposes and the contiguous copy entirely**.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Workloads and problem sizes\n\n| # | uuid (short) | B  | S    | M=B·S | atol   | rtol |\n|---|--------------|----|------|-------|--------|------|\n| 1 | b0a9e3f0     | 1  | 1024 | 1024  | 0.0130 | 0.05 |\n| 2 | 8b678b13     | 4  | 256  | 1024  | 0.0130 | 0.05 |\n| 3 | 58e3ac47     | 32 | 256  | 8192  | 0.0210 | 0.05 |\n| 4 | 6033fd0f     | 2  | 4096 | 8192  | 0.0210 | 0.05 |\n| 5 | bb262a64     | 1  | 2048 | 2048  | 0.0098 | 0.05 |\n\nObservations:\n- `H=2048`, `3H=6144`, `K_c=4`, `groups=H` are **compile-time constants** — bake them in and\n  specialize kernels / `tl.constexpr`.\n- Effective GEMM shapes: **GEMM1** is `(M×2048) · (2048×6144)`; **GEMM2** is `(M×2048) · (2048×2048)`.\n- `M` takes only **three distinct values** {1024, 2048, 8192}. `S` takes {256,1024,2048,4096}. Small\n  distinct set → autotune configs will generalize well; `M` is always a multiple of 256 and 1024,\n  and `S` is always a power-of-two ≥ 256 (helpful for conv tiling / no ragged masks on `M` within a batch).\n- Per-workload compute (FLOPs): GEMM1 ≈ `2·M·2048·6144`, GEMM2 ≈ `2·M·2048·2048` → **GEMM1 is 3×\n  GEMM2**. The middle (gating+conv+gating) is tiny FLOPs but touches `~5·M·H` bf16 elements → memory-bound.\n- rtol 5% is loose; atol is the tighter constraint (0.0098 on WL5, 0.013 on WL1/2, 0.021 on WL3/4).\n  WL5 (B=1, S=2048, atol 0.0098) is the least forgiving.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Constraints & environment realities\n\n- **Triton-only compute.** Both GEMMs must be Triton `tl.dot` kernels (cannot call `F.linear`/`torch.matmul`\n  for compute — that would be a Torch computational fallback). This is the central performance risk:\n  we must approach cuBLAS bf16 throughput on Ampere with a well-tuned Triton matmul, then win the rest\n  via fusion.\n- **No local execution / no profiler.** Per CLAUDE.md we may not run CUDA, a profiler, `nvidia-smi`,\n  the external evaluator directly, or any alternate correctness harness. The **only** feedback channel\n  is `./scripts/evaluate_candidate.sh feedback cNNN`, and each such run (5 workloads) consumes **one**\n  of 100 evals and reports correctness + speedup. ⇒ correctness must be established **analytically**\n  before spending an eval; a failed Triton run is wasted budget.\n- **Skills:** `KernelWiki` targets Blackwell/Hopper (tcgen05/TMEM/CLC/FP4) and is **not applicable** to\n  `sm_80`; `ncu-report-skill` requires running the profiler, which is disallowed. Both are effectively\n  unusable here beyond generic reading; note in candidate `skill_usage` accordingly.\n- **Immutability:** every meaningful source/config/launch change ⇒ new `cNNN`; never rewrite past records.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Numerical analysis & risks\n\n### 4.1 Reference precision chain\nReference rounds to **bf16 at every stage**: `BCx` (fp32-accum cuBLAS → bf16), `Bx = Bgate*Xproj`\n(bf16 op, internally fp32→round bf16), `conv_out` (cuDNN bf16, fp32-accum → bf16), `y = Cgate*conv_out`\n(→ bf16), then GEMM2 (fp32-accum → bf16). So the \"ground truth\" itself carries bf16 rounding at 4\nintermediate points.\n\n### 4.2 Our precision plan\n- **GEMMs:** load bf16 operands, accumulate in **fp32** (`tl.dot(..., out_dtype=fp32)` / fp32\n  accumulator), add bias in fp32, cast to bf16 on store. Matches cuBLAS behavior.\n- **Middle (gating+conv+gating):** compute entirely in **fp32** (promote loaded bf16 → fp32, multiply,\n  conv-accumulate in fp32, multiply, then cast the final `y` to bf16). This is *more* accurate than the\n  reference's intermediate bf16 roundings, and the differences are well inside the loose tolerances.\n  - Key subtlety: the reference rounds `Bx` to **bf16 before** the conv. If our fp32 path ever drifts\n    outside atol we can insert an explicit `Bx_bf16 = Bx.to(bf16).to(fp32)` round to mirror the\n    reference; keep this as a fallback lever (WL5 with atol 0.0098 is where to watch).\n- **Accumulation order:** conv is only 4 taps → order-insensitive. GEMM2 reduces over `H=2048`; use\n  the standard `BLOCK_K` split with fp32 accumulator (cuBLAS also splits K). Differences here are\n  random-sign and bounded by rtol 0.05 over 2048 terms.\n\n### 4.3 Correctness pitfalls to get right the first time\n1. **Tap order / causal direction:** `k=3` is the current sample; taps go backward in `s`. A mirrored\n   kernel (using `conv_weight[...,K_c-1-k]` or reading `s+k`) will silently pass shape checks but fail\n   numerically. Derive from `F.pad(left=3)` + `conv1d` cross-correlation semantics (PyTorch conv1d is\n   cross-correlation, **not** flipped convolution).\n2. **Batch boundaries:** taps at `s-3+k<0` must be zero; never read row `m-3+k` from the previous batch.\n   Guard with `s - 3 + k ≥ 0` (equivalently `row ≥ b*S`).\n3. **Chunk offsets:** `Bgate=[0,H)`, `Cgate=[H,2H)`, `Xproj=[2H,3H)` — off-by-`H` here is a classic bug.\n4. **`F.linear` transpose:** weights are `(out, in)`; GEMM computes `Σ_in x·W[out,in]` (i.e. `x @ W^T`).\n5. **Bias dtype:** biases are bf16; add in fp32 then round.\n6. **Contiguity of `x`:** reshape `(B,S,H)→(M,H)` is only free if `x` is contiguous (it is per definition);\n   assert strides / use `.reshape` guarded, but do not silently `.contiguous()` an already-contiguous tensor.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Triton design space\n\nBaseline decomposition into **3 Triton kernels** (all in `(M,·)` space, no transposes):\n\n### K1 — GEMM1 with bias: `BCx = x @ in_proj_weight^T + in_proj_bias`, shape `(M, 3H)`\n- Standard tiled bf16 matmul: `BLOCK_M × BLOCK_N` output tile, `BLOCK_K` reduction over `H=2048`,\n  fp32 accumulator, L2-friendly `GROUP_M` row-grouping, `num_warps`/`num_stages` autotuned.\n- `N=6144` and `K=2048` are multiples of 64/128 → no N/K masking; `M∈{1024,2048,8192}` multiples of\n  128 → no M masking for `BLOCK_M∈{64,128,256}`. Clean, mask-free fast path.\n- Candidate tile menu: `BLOCK_M∈{64,128,256}`, `BLOCK_N∈{64,128,256}`, `BLOCK_K∈{32,64,128}`,\n  `num_stages∈{3,4,5}`, `num_warps∈{4,8}`, `GROUP_M∈{4,8}`.\n\n### K2 — fused gating + causal depthwise conv + gating: `y (M, H)`\n- Reads `BCx`, computes `Bx=BCx[:, :H]*BCx[:, 2H:3H]`, the 4-tap causal conv (per-channel weights\n  `conv_weight[h,0,·]`, `conv_bias[h]`), then `y = BCx[:, H:2H] * conv_out`. Pure element-wise + tiny\n  stencil ⇒ **memory-bound**; goal is minimum passes.\n- Tiling options:\n  - (a) **Sequence-tiled**: block `(BLOCK_S rows within one batch) × (BLOCK_H channels)`; load a\n    halo of 3 extra rows above the tile, compute `Bx` once, do the 4-tap stencil in registers/smem.\n    Amortizes the `Bx` computation and the neighbor reads. Grid = `(B, ceil(S/BLOCK_S), ceil(H/BLOCK_H))`.\n  - (b) **Row-parallel** (simple): one program per `(row-block, channel-block)`, recompute the ≤4\n    neighbor `Bx` values by re-loading `Bgate,Xproj` for rows `m-3..m`. Simplest to prove correct;\n    up to 4× redundant loads of `Bgate/Xproj` but conv is cheap. Good **c001** choice.\n  - Batch-boundary guard via `s` index; conv weights broadcast over the row dimension (per-channel).\n- Because `conv_weight`/`conv_bias` are `(H,·)` and reused for all `M` rows, keep them resident per\n  channel-block.\n\n### K3 — GEMM2 with bias: `output = y @ out_proj_weight^T + out_proj_bias`, shape `(M, H)`\n- Same matmul template as K1 with `N=2048`.\n\n### Fusion escalation ladder (later candidates)\n- **F1: fold gating into GEMM1 epilogue.** Produce only `Bx (M,H)` and `Cgate (M,H)` (write `2H`\n  instead of `3H`) by combining the `[0,H)` and `[2H,3H)` column tiles in the epilogue. Needs the\n  epilogue to see both column blocks → either `BLOCK_N` spanning matched columns or a two-pass\n  accumulator; evaluate cost/benefit (saves ~1/3 of the intermediate write+read).\n- **F2: fuse conv into GEMM2's A-load.** GEMM2's `A = y (M,H)`; `y[m,h]` needs `conv_out[m,h]` which\n  needs neighbor rows `m-1..m-3`. Feasible only if `BLOCK_M` rows are contiguous in `M` and we load a\n  3-row halo into the K-loop; complex and risks correctness at batch/tile edges. Treat as a stretch goal\n  only if K2 is shown to be a real bottleneck.\n- **F3: merge K1+K2** so the gating/conv is the GEMM1 epilogue producing `y` directly. Blocked by the\n  conv needing cross-row (cross-`BLOCK_M`) neighbors; likely not worth it. Keep K2 standalone.\n- **GEMM1 restructuring:** single `N=3H` GEMM reuses each `A` tile across all three projections (best A\n  reuse) — prefer over three separate GEMMs.\n\n### Where the time goes (rough Ampere model, M=8192 case)\n- GEMM1 ≈ `2·8192·2048·6144 ≈ 2.06e11` FLOP; GEMM2 ≈ `6.9e10` FLOP; total ≈ `2.75e11`.\n- At ~1.5e14 bf16 FLOP/s realized → ~1.8 ms of unavoidable matmul; K2 memory traffic\n  (`~5·M·H·2 B ≈ 0.17 GB`) at ~1.5 TB/s → ~0.1 ms. **GEMMs dominate.**\n- ⇒ The realistic upside is: (i) our Triton GEMMs must land near cuBLAS, and (ii) we recover the\n  reference's overheads — the slow **cuDNN depthwise `conv1d` with groups=2048**, two transposes, a\n  `.contiguous()` copy, and several extra kernel launches / intermediate allocations. Grouped/depthwise\n  conv over 2048 groups is a known cuDNN weak spot; that plus the transposes is our headroom.\n- **Speedup expectation:** modest, roughly **1.1–1.5×** if GEMMs match cuBLAS; **<1×** risk if the\n  Triton GEMMs underperform cuBLAS by more than the fusion savings. This risk ordering drives the\n  candidate roadmap: first prove correctness + measure the GEMM gap, then tune GEMMs, then tighten fusion.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Validation strategy (given no local execution)\n\n1. **Analytical correctness before any eval.** Re-derive every index map (§1.1, §4.3) and shape on\n   paper; write kernels with `constexpr` H/3H/K_c so shapes are self-documenting. Add cheap host-side\n   `assert`s on dtypes/shapes/contiguity in `run(...)` (metadata only — allowed).\n2. **Start simple (c001 = correctness anchor):** 3 straightforward kernels (K1, K2 row-parallel variant\n   b, K3), conservative single tile config, fp32 middle. The goal of c001 is a **passing, well-defined\n   baseline** and a first speedup number, not peak performance.\n3. **One variable per candidate.** After c001 passes, change exactly one thing per new `cNNN`\n   (autotune GEMM1, then GEMM2, then K2 sequence-tiling, then F1 fusion), so each eval attributes its\n   delta cleanly. Record parent/hash/hypothesis/per-workload result/geomean/decision/eval-count.\n4. **Correctness watch-list:** WL5 (atol 0.0098) is the tightest — if a candidate fails only there,\n   suspect the fp32-vs-bf16 `Bx` rounding (§4.2) or a conv tap/edge bug, not the GEMMs.\n5. **Budget discipline:** 100 evals but token budget is the likely binding constraint; avoid\n   speculative evals. Converge when geomean gains stall, then write `SEARCH_COMPLETE`. Never run `final`\n   without operator approval.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Open questions to resolve empirically (via feedback evals)\n- How close does a tuned Triton bf16 matmul get to cuBLAS on A800 for these `M,N,K`? (Sets the ceiling.)\n- How large is the reference's `conv1d(groups=2048)`+transpose+contiguous overhead as a fraction of\n  total? (Sets our headroom.) Inferred only from the end-to-end speedup deltas across candidates.\n- Is K2 better as sequence-tiled (halo reuse) or row-parallel (simple)? Decide after c001’s number.\n- Does F1 (gating-in-epilogue) pay for its added complexity, or is the standalone memory-bound K2\n  already negligible vs the GEMMs?\n\n## 8. Immediate next step\nWrite `docs/plan.md` with the executable candidate roadmap (c001 correctness anchor → GEMM autotune →\nK2 tiling → fusion escalation), then implement `c001`.\n","numLines":240,"startLine":1,"totalLines":240}}}
