{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Y3r4DRzEzLHiZ9DvKHCNeV","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Draft — L1/005 `conv_gated_projection_with_causal_conv`\n2\t\n3\tTarget HW: NVIDIA **A800 (`sm_80`, Ampere)**. Primary impl **must be Triton**; PyTorch only for\n4\tmetadata/launch plumbing. No Torch/CPU/NumPy/CUDA-ext computational fallback. Metric: **geomean\n5\tspeedup vs the reference across the 5 fixed feedback workloads, with every selected workload passing\n6\tcorrectness**. Budget: 100 candidate evals; token soft 1.0M / hard 1.2M.\n7\t\n8\t---\n9\t\n10\t## 1. What the operation computes\n11\t\n12\tSignature (all tensors `bfloat16`), constants `H = hidden_size = 2048`, `K_c = conv_kernel_size = 4`,\n13\t`3H = triple_hidden = 6144`:\n14\t\n15\t```\n16\tx               : (B, S, H)\n17\tin_proj_weight  : (3H, H)      in_proj_bias : (3H,)\n18\tconv_weight     : (H, 1, K_c)  conv_bias    : (H,)      # depthwise, groups = H\n19\tout_proj_weight : (H, H)       out_proj_bias: (H,)\n20\t-> output       : (B, S, H)\n21\t```\n22\t\n23\tReference flow (from `task/definition.json`):\n24\t\n25\t1. `BCx = F.linear(x, in_proj_weight, in_proj_bias)` → `(B, S, 3H)`  *(GEMM1)*\n26\t2. transpose to `(B, 3H, S)`, `chunk(3, dim=1)` → `Bgate, Cgate, Xproj`, each `(B, H, S)`\n27\t3. `Bx = Bgate * Xproj`  *(element-wise gating)*\n28\t4. `Bx_padded = F.pad(Bx, (K_c-1, 0))` then `F.conv1d(..., groups=H)` → `conv_out (B,H,S)` *(depthwise causal conv)*\n29\t5. `y = Cgate * conv_out`  *(output gating)*\n30\t6. transpose back to `(B, S, H)`, `.contiguous()`\n31\t7. `output = F.linear(y, out_proj_weight, out_proj_bias)` *(GEMM2)*\n32\t\n33\t### 1.1 Exact index mapping (critical for correctness)\n34\t\n35\tFlatten rows as `m = b*S + s`, with `M = B*S`, `m ∈ [0,M)`, `s = m mod S`, `b = m // S`.\n36\t\n37\tAfter the transpose+chunk, for output channel `h ∈ [0,H)`:\n38\t- `Bgate[b,h,s] = BCx[b,s, 0*H + h]`\n39\t- `Cgate[b,h,s] = BCx[b,s, 1*H + h]`\n40\t- `Xproj[b,h,s] = BCx[b,s, 2*H + h]`\n41\t\n42\tSo the three chunks are **contiguous column blocks** of `BCx`: `[0,H)`, `[H,2H)`, `[2H,3H)`.\n43\t\n44\tGating: `Bx[m,h] = BCx[m,h] * BCx[m, 2H+h]`.\n45\t\n46\tCausal depthwise conv (`F.pad` left = `K_c-1 = 3`, no right pad, stride 1, kernel 4). For tap\n47\t`k ∈ {0,1,2,3}` the padded input aligns so that output position `s` reads `Bx` at position `s-3+k`:\n48\t\n49\t```\n50\tconv_out[m,h] = conv_bias[h] + Σ_{k=0..3} conv_weight[h,0,k] * Bx[b, h, s-3+k]\n51\t              where Bx[b,h,·] taps at s-3+k are ZERO when (s-3+k) < 0   (causal left pad)\n52\t```\n53\t\n54\t- Tap `k=3` = **current** position `s` (weight `conv_weight[h,0,3]`).\n55\t- Tap `k=0` = oldest position `s-3` (weight `conv_weight[h,0,0]`).\n56\t- In flattened rows, position `s-3+k` is row `m-3+k`, valid only while it stays **in the same batch**\n57\t  (`s-3+k ≥ 0`). Rows crossing below `s=0` are the causal zero pad → must be masked to 0. Because rows\n58\t  are laid out `b*S + s`, staying `≥ b*S` is exactly the `s-3+k ≥ 0` condition, so the batch boundary\n59\t  and the causal pad coincide — a single `(s-3+k) ≥ 0` guard handles both.\n60\t\n61\tOutput gating: `y[m,h] = BCx[m, H+h] * conv_out[m,h]`.\n62\t\n63\tGEMM2 (note `F.linear` uses `W^T`): `output[m, n] = out_proj_bias[n] + Σ_{h} y[m,h] * out_proj_weight[n,h]`.\n64\t\n65\tLikewise GEMM1: `BCx[m, j] = in_proj_bias[j] + Σ_{i} x[m,i] * in_proj_weight[j,i]`.\n66\t\n67\t**Layout win:** everything lives in `(M, ·)` row-major space (`x → (M,H)` is a free view of the\n68\t`(B,S,H)` contiguous tensor; `y → (M,H)`). The reference's two `transpose`s and `.contiguous()` copy\n69\tare only artifacts of routing data through `conv1d`'s `(B,C,S)` convention. If we run the conv along\n70\t`M`-rows directly we can **eliminate both transposes and the contiguous copy entirely**.\n71\t\n72\t---\n73\t\n74\t## 2. Workloads and problem sizes\n75\t\n76\t| # | uuid (short) | B  | S    | M=B·S | atol   | rtol |\n77\t|---|--------------|----|------|-------|--------|------|\n78\t| 1 | b0a9e3f0     | 1  | 1024 | 1024  | 0.0130 | 0.05 |\n79\t| 2 | 8b678b13     | 4  | 256  | 1024  | 0.0130 | 0.05 |\n80\t| 3 | 58e3ac47     | 32 | 256  | 8192  | 0.0210 | 0.05 |\n81\t| 4 | 6033fd0f     | 2  | 4096 | 8192  | 0.0210 | 0.05 |\n82\t| 5 | bb262a64     | 1  | 2048 | 2048  | 0.0098 | 0.05 |\n83\t\n84\tObservations:\n85\t- `H=2048`, `3H=6144`, `K_c=4`, `groups=H` are **compile-time constants** — bake them in and\n86\t  specialize kernels / `tl.constexpr`.\n87\t- Effective GEMM shapes: **GEMM1** is `(M×2048) · (2048×6144)`; **GEMM2** is `(M×2048) · (2048×2048)`.\n88\t- `M` takes only **three distinct values** {1024, 2048, 8192}. `S` takes {256,1024,2048,4096}. Small\n89\t  distinct set → autotune configs will generalize well; `M` is always a multiple of 256 and 1024,\n90\t  and `S` is always a power-of-two ≥ 256 (helpful for conv tiling / no ragged masks on `M` within a batch).\n91\t- Per-workload compute (FLOPs): GEMM1 ≈ `2·M·2048·6144`, GEMM2 ≈ `2·M·2048·2048` → **GEMM1 is 3×\n92\t  GEMM2**. The middle (gating+conv+gating) is tiny FLOPs but touches `~5·M·H` bf16 elements → memory-bound.\n93\t- rtol 5% is loose; atol is the tighter constraint (0.0098 on WL5, 0.013 on WL1/2, 0.021 on WL3/4).\n94\t  WL5 (B=1, S=2048, atol 0.0098) is the least forgiving.\n95\t\n96\t---\n97\t\n98\t## 3. Constraints & environment realities\n99\t\n100\t- **Triton-only compute.** Both GEMMs must be Triton `tl.dot` kernels (cannot call `F.linear`/`torch.matmul`\n101\t  for compute — that would be a Torch computational fallback). This is the central performance risk:\n102\t  we must approach cuBLAS bf16 throughput on Ampere with a well-tuned Triton matmul, then win the rest\n103\t  via fusion.\n104\t- **No local execution / no profiler.** Per CLAUDE.md we may not run CUDA, a profiler, `nvidia-smi`,\n105\t  the external evaluator directly, or any alternate correctness harness. The **only** feedback channel\n106\t  is `./scripts/evaluate_candidate.sh feedback cNNN`, and each such run (5 workloads) consumes **one**\n107\t  of 100 evals and reports correctness + speedup. ⇒ correctness must be established **analytically**\n108\t  before spending an eval; a failed Triton run is wasted budget.\n109\t- **Skills:** `KernelWiki` targets Blackwell/Hopper (tcgen05/TMEM/CLC/FP4) and is **not applicable** to\n110\t  `sm_80`; `ncu-report-skill` requires running the profiler, which is disallowed. Both are effectively\n111\t  unusable here beyond generic reading; note in candidate `skill_usage` accordingly.\n112\t- **Immutability:** every meaningful source/config/launch change ⇒ new `cNNN`; never rewrite past records.\n113\t\n114\t---\n115\t\n116\t## 4. Numerical analysis & risks\n117\t\n118\t### 4.1 Reference precision chain\n119\tReference rounds to **bf16 at every stage**: `BCx` (fp32-accum cuBLAS → bf16), `Bx = Bgate*Xproj`\n120\t(bf16 op, internally fp32→round bf16), `conv_out` (cuDNN bf16, fp32-accum → bf16), `y = Cgate*conv_out`\n121\t(→ bf16), then GEMM2 (fp32-accum → bf16). So the \"ground truth\" itself carries bf16 rounding at 4\n122\tintermediate points.\n123\t\n124\t### 4.2 Our precision plan\n125\t- **GEMMs:** load bf16 operands, accumulate in **fp32** (`tl.dot(..., out_dtype=fp32)` / fp32\n126\t  accumulator), add bias in fp32, cast to bf16 on store. Matches cuBLAS behavior.\n127\t- **Middle (gating+conv+gating):** compute entirely in **fp32** (promote loaded bf16 → fp32, multiply,\n128\t  conv-accumulate in fp32, multiply, then cast the final `y` to bf16). This is *more* accurate than the\n129\t  reference's intermediate bf16 roundings, and the differences are well inside the loose tolerances.\n130\t  - Key subtlety: the reference rounds `Bx` to **bf16 before** the conv. If our fp32 path ever drifts\n131\t    outside atol we can insert an explicit `Bx_bf16 = Bx.to(bf16).to(fp32)` round to mirror the\n132\t    reference; keep this as a fallback lever (WL5 with atol 0.0098 is where to watch).\n133\t- **Accumulation order:** conv is only 4 taps → order-insensitive. GEMM2 reduces over `H=2048`; use\n134\t  the standard `BLOCK_K` split with fp32 accumulator (cuBLAS also splits K). Differences here are\n135\t  random-sign and bounded by rtol 0.05 over 2048 terms.\n136\t\n137\t### 4.3 Correctness pitfalls to get right the first time\n138\t1. **Tap order / causal direction:** `k=3` is the current sample; taps go backward in `s`. A mirrored\n139\t   kernel (using `conv_weight[...,K_c-1-k]` or reading `s+k`) will silently pass shape checks but fail\n140\t   numerically. Derive from `F.pad(left=3)` + `conv1d` cross-correlation semantics (PyTorch conv1d is\n141\t   cross-correlation, **not** flipped convolution).\n142\t2. **Batch boundaries:** taps at `s-3+k<0` must be zero; never read row `m-3+k` from the previous batch.\n143\t   Guard with `s - 3 + k ≥ 0` (equivalently `row ≥ b*S`).\n144\t3. **Chunk offsets:** `Bgate=[0,H)`, `Cgate=[H,2H)`, `Xproj=[2H,3H)` — off-by-`H` here is a classic bug.\n145\t4. **`F.linear` transpose:** weights are `(out, in)`; GEMM computes `Σ_in x·W[out,in]` (i.e. `x @ W^T`).\n146\t5. **Bias dtype:** biases are bf16; add in fp32 then round.\n147\t6. **Contiguity of `x`:** reshape `(B,S,H)→(M,H)` is only free if `x` is contiguous (it is per definition);\n148\t   assert strides / use `.reshape` guarded, but do not silently `.contiguous()` an already-contiguous tensor.\n149\t\n150\t---\n151\t\n152\t## 5. Triton design space\n153\t\n154\tBaseline decomposition into **3 Triton kernels** (all in `(M,·)` space, no transposes):\n155\t\n156\t### K1 — GEMM1 with bias: `BCx = x @ in_proj_weight^T + in_proj_bias`, shape `(M, 3H)`\n157\t- Standard tiled bf16 matmul: `BLOCK_M × BLOCK_N` output tile, `BLOCK_K` reduction over `H=2048`,\n158\t  fp32 accumulator, L2-friendly `GROUP_M` row-grouping, `num_warps`/`num_stages` autotuned.\n159\t- `N=6144` and `K=2048` are multiples of 64/128 → no N/K masking; `M∈{1024,2048,8192}` multiples of\n160\t  128 → no M masking for `BLOCK_M∈{64,128,256}`. Clean, mask-free fast path.\n161\t- Candidate tile menu: `BLOCK_M∈{64,128,256}`, `BLOCK_N∈{64,128,256}`, `BLOCK_K∈{32,64,128}`,\n162\t  `num_stages∈{3,4,5}`, `num_warps∈{4,8}`, `GROUP_M∈{4,8}`.\n163\t\n164\t### K2 — fused gating + causal depthwise conv + gating: `y (M, H)`\n165\t- Reads `BCx`, computes `Bx=BCx[:, :H]*BCx[:, 2H:3H]`, the 4-tap causal conv (per-channel weights\n166\t  `conv_weight[h,0,·]`, `conv_bias[h]`), then `y = BCx[:, H:2H] * conv_out`. Pure element-wise + tiny\n167\t  stencil ⇒ **memory-bound**; goal is minimum passes.\n168\t- Tiling options:\n169\t  - (a) **Sequence-tiled**: block `(BLOCK_S rows within one batch) × (BLOCK_H channels)`; load a\n170\t    halo of 3 extra rows above the tile, compute `Bx` once, do the 4-tap stencil in registers/smem.\n171\t    Amortizes the `Bx` computation and the neighbor reads. Grid = `(B, ceil(S/BLOCK_S), ceil(H/BLOCK_H))`.\n172\t  - (b) **Row-parallel** (simple): one program per `(row-block, channel-block)`, recompute the ≤4\n173\t    neighbor `Bx` values by re-loading `Bgate,Xproj` for rows `m-3..m`. Simplest to prove correct;\n174\t    up to 4× redundant loads of `Bgate/Xproj` but conv is cheap. Good **c001** choice.\n175\t  - Batch-boundary guard via `s` index; conv weights broadcast over the row dimension (per-channel).\n176\t- Because `conv_weight`/`conv_bias` are `(H,·)` and reused for all `M` rows, keep them resident per\n177\t  channel-block.\n178\t\n179\t### K3 — GEMM2 with bias: `output = y @ out_proj_weight^T + out_proj_bias`, shape `(M, H)`\n180\t- Same matmul template as K1 with `N=2048`.\n181\t\n182\t### Fusion escalation ladder (later candidates)\n183\t- **F1: fold gating into GEMM1 epilogue.** Produce only `Bx (M,H)` and `Cgate (M,H)` (write `2H`\n184\t  instead of `3H`) by combining the `[0,H)` and `[2H,3H)` column tiles in the epilogue. Needs the\n185\t  epilogue to see both column blocks → either `BLOCK_N` spanning matched columns or a two-pass\n186\t  accumulator; evaluate cost/benefit (saves ~1/3 of the intermediate write+read).\n187\t- **F2: fuse conv into GEMM2's A-load.** GEMM2's `A = y (M,H)`; `y[m,h]` needs `conv_out[m,h]` which\n188\t  needs neighbor rows `m-1..m-3`. Feasible only if `BLOCK_M` rows are contiguous in `M` and we load a\n189\t  3-row halo into the K-loop; complex and risks correctness at batch/tile edges. Treat as a stretch goal\n190\t  only if K2 is shown to be a real bottleneck.\n191\t- **F3: merge K1+K2** so the gating/conv is the GEMM1 epilogue producing `y` directly. Blocked by the\n192\t  conv needing cross-row (cross-`BLOCK_M`) neighbors; likely not worth it. Keep K2 standalone.\n193\t- **GEMM1 restructuring:** single `N=3H` GEMM reuses each `A` tile across all three projections (best A\n194\t  reuse) — prefer over three separate GEMMs.\n195\t\n196\t### Where the time goes (rough Ampere model, M=8192 case)\n197\t- GEMM1 ≈ `2·8192·2048·6144 ≈ 2.06e11` FLOP; GEMM2 ≈ `6.9e10` FLOP; total ≈ `2.75e11`.\n198\t- At ~1.5e14 bf16 FLOP/s realized → ~1.8 ms of unavoidable matmul; K2 memory traffic\n199\t  (`~5·M·H·2 B ≈ 0.17 GB`) at ~1.5 TB/s → ~0.1 ms. **GEMMs dominate.**\n200\t- ⇒ The realistic upside is: (i) our Triton GEMMs must land near cuBLAS, and (ii) we recover the\n201\t  reference's overheads — the slow **cuDNN depthwise `conv1d` with groups=2048**, two transposes, a\n202\t  `.contiguous()` copy, and several extra kernel launches / intermediate allocations. Grouped/depthwise\n203\t  conv over 2048 groups is a known cuDNN weak spot; that plus the transposes is our headroom.\n204\t- **Speedup expectation:** modest, roughly **1.1–1.5×** if GEMMs match cuBLAS; **<1×** risk if the\n205\t  Triton GEMMs underperform cuBLAS by more than the fusion savings. This risk ordering drives the\n206\t  candidate roadmap: first prove correctness + measure the GEMM gap, then tune GEMMs, then tighten fusion.\n207\t\n208\t---\n209\t\n210\t## 6. Validation strategy (given no local execution)\n211\t\n212\t1. **Analytical correctness before any eval.** Re-derive every index map (§1.1, §4.3) and shape on\n213\t   paper; write kernels with `constexpr` H/3H/K_c so shapes are self-documenting. Add cheap host-side\n214\t   `assert`s on dtypes/shapes/contiguity in `run(...)` (metadata only — allowed).\n215\t2. **Start simple (c001 = correctness anchor):** 3 straightforward kernels (K1, K2 row-parallel variant\n216\t   b, K3), conservative single tile config, fp32 middle. The goal of c001 is a **passing, well-defined\n217\t   baseline** and a first speedup number, not peak performance.\n218\t3. **One variable per candidate.** After c001 passes, change exactly one thing per new `cNNN`\n219\t   (autotune GEMM1, then GEMM2, then K2 sequence-tiling, then F1 fusion), so each eval attributes its\n220\t   delta cleanly. Record parent/hash/hypothesis/per-workload result/geomean/decision/eval-count.\n221\t4. **Correctness watch-list:** WL5 (atol 0.0098) is the tightest — if a candidate fails only there,\n222\t   suspect the fp32-vs-bf16 `Bx` rounding (§4.2) or a conv tap/edge bug, not the GEMMs.\n223\t5. **Budget discipline:** 100 evals but token budget is the likely binding constraint; avoid\n224\t   speculative evals. Converge when geomean gains stall, then write `SEARCH_COMPLETE`. Never run `final`\n225\t   without operator approval.\n226\t\n227\t---\n228\t\n229\t## 7. Open questions to resolve empirically (via feedback evals)\n230\t- How close does a tuned Triton bf16 matmul get to cuBLAS on A800 for these `M,N,K`? (Sets the ceiling.)\n231\t- How large is the reference's `conv1d(groups=2048)`+transpose+contiguous overhead as a fraction of\n232\t  total? (Sets our headroom.) Inferred only from the end-to-end speedup deltas across candidates.\n233\t- Is K2 better as sequence-tiled (halo reuse) or row-parallel (simple)? Decide after c001’s number.\n234\t- Does F1 (gating-in-epilogue) pay for its added complexity, or is the standalone memory-bound K2\n235\t  already negligible vs the GEMMs?\n236\t\n237\t## 8. Immediate next step\n238\tWrite `docs/plan.md` with the executable candidate roadmap (c001 correctness anchor → GEMM autotune →\n239\tK2 tiling → fusion escalation), then implement `c001`.\n240\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b8e044ef-369c-4078-b4f1-f9181e49de1a","uuid":"a03ebd30-0858-4adb-b400-558e6c57b19f","timestamp":"2026-09-16T17:25:35.921Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/data1/workspace/weihongren/kda-runs/formal-kda-20260916--sol_execbench--L1-005_conv_gated_projection_with_causal_conv/docs/draft.md","content":"# Draft — L1/005 `conv_gated_projection_with_causal_conv`\n\nTarget HW: NVIDIA **A800 (`sm_80`, Ampere)**. Primary impl **must be Triton**; PyTorch only for\nmetadata/launch plumbing. No Torch/CPU/NumPy/CUDA-ext computational fallback. Metric: **geomean\nspeedup vs the reference across the 5 fixed feedback workloads, with every selected workload passing\ncorrectness**. Budget: 100 candidate evals; token soft 1.0M / hard 1.2M.\n\n---\n\n## 1. What the operation computes\n\nSignature (all tensors `bfloat16`), constants `H = hidden_size = 2048`, `K_c = conv_kernel_size = 4`,\n`3H = triple_hidden = 6144`:\n\n```\nx               : (B, S, H)\nin_proj_weight  : (3H, H)      in_proj_bias : (3H,)\nconv_weight     : (H, 1, K_c)  conv_bias    : (H,)      # depthwise, groups = H\nout_proj_weight : (H, H)       out_proj_bias: (H,)\n-> output       : (B, S, H)\n```\n\nReference flow (from `task/definition.json`):\n\n1. `BCx = F.linear(x, in_proj_weight, in_proj_bias)` → `(B, S, 3H)`  *(GEMM1)*\n2. transpose to `(B, 3H, S)`, `chunk(3, dim=1)` → `Bgate, Cgate, Xproj`, each `(B, H, S)`\n3. `Bx = Bgate * Xproj`  *(element-wise gating)*\n4. `Bx_padded = F.pad(Bx, (K_c-1, 0))` then `F.conv1d(..., groups=H)` → `conv_out (B,H,S)` *(depthwise causal conv)*\n5. `y = Cgate * conv_out`  *(output gating)*\n6. transpose back to `(B, S, H)`, `.contiguous()`\n7. `output = F.linear(y, out_proj_weight, out_proj_bias)` *(GEMM2)*\n\n### 1.1 Exact index mapping (critical for correctness)\n\nFlatten rows as `m = b*S + s`, with `M = B*S`, `m ∈ [0,M)`, `s = m mod S`, `b = m // S`.\n\nAfter the transpose+chunk, for output channel `h ∈ [0,H)`:\n- `Bgate[b,h,s] = BCx[b,s, 0*H + h]`\n- `Cgate[b,h,s] = BCx[b,s, 1*H + h]`\n- `Xproj[b,h,s] = BCx[b,s, 2*H + h]`\n\nSo the three chunks are **contiguous column blocks** of `BCx`: `[0,H)`, `[H,2H)`, `[2H,3H)`.\n\nGating: `Bx[m,h] = BCx[m,h] * BCx[m, 2H+h]`.\n\nCausal depthwise conv (`F.pad` left = `K_c-1 = 3`, no right pad, stride 1, kernel 4). For tap\n`k ∈ {0,1,2,3}` the padded input aligns so that output position `s` reads `Bx` at position `s-3+k`:\n\n```\nconv_out[m,h] = conv_bias[h] + Σ_{k=0..3} conv_weight[h,0,k] * Bx[b, h, s-3+k]\n              where Bx[b,h,·] taps at s-3+k are ZERO when (s-3+k) < 0   (causal left pad)\n```\n\n- Tap `k=3` = **current** position `s` (weight `conv_weight[h,0,3]`).\n- Tap `k=0` = oldest position `s-3` (weight `conv_weight[h,0,0]`).\n- In flattened rows, position `s-3+k` is row `m-3+k`, valid only while it stays **in the same batch**\n  (`s-3+k ≥ 0`). Rows crossing below `s=0` are the causal zero pad → must be masked to 0. Because rows\n  are laid out `b*S + s`, staying `≥ b*S` is exactly the `s-3+k ≥ 0` condition, so the batch boundary\n  and the causal pad coincide — a single `(s-3+k) ≥ 0` guard handles both.\n\nOutput gating: `y[m,h] = BCx[m, H+h] * conv_out[m,h]`.\n\nGEMM2 (note `F.linear` uses `W^T`): `output[m, n] = out_proj_bias[n] + Σ_{h} y[m,h] * out_proj_weight[n,h]`.\n\nLikewise GEMM1: `BCx[m, j] = in_proj_bias[j] + Σ_{i} x[m,i] * in_proj_weight[j,i]`.\n\n**Layout win:** everything lives in `(M, ·)` row-major space (`x → (M,H)` is a free view of the\n`(B,S,H)` contiguous tensor; `y → (M,H)`). The reference's two `transpose`s and `.contiguous()` copy\nare only artifacts of routing data through `conv1d`'s `(B,C,S)` convention. If we run the conv along\n`M`-rows directly we can **eliminate both transposes and the contiguous copy entirely**.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Workloads and problem sizes\n\n| # | uuid (short) | B  | S    | M=B·S | atol   | rtol |\n|---|--------------|----|------|-------|--------|------|\n| 1 | b0a9e3f0     | 1  | 1024 | 1024  | 0.0130 | 0.05 |\n| 2 | 8b678b13     | 4  | 256  | 1024  | 0.0130 | 0.05 |\n| 3 | 58e3ac47     | 32 | 256  | 8192  | 0.0210 | 0.05 |\n| 4 | 6033fd0f     | 2  | 4096 | 8192  | 0.0210 | 0.05 |\n| 5 | bb262a64     | 1  | 2048 | 2048  | 0.0098 | 0.05 |\n\nObservations:\n- `H=2048`, `3H=6144`, `K_c=4`, `groups=H` are **compile-time constants** — bake them in and\n  specialize kernels / `tl.constexpr`.\n- Effective GEMM shapes: **GEMM1** is `(M×2048) · (2048×6144)`; **GEMM2** is `(M×2048) · (2048×2048)`.\n- `M` takes only **three distinct values** {1024, 2048, 8192}. `S` takes {256,1024,2048,4096}. Small\n  distinct set → autotune configs will generalize well; `M` is always a multiple of 256 and 1024,\n  and `S` is always a power-of-two ≥ 256 (helpful for conv tiling / no ragged masks on `M` within a batch).\n- Per-workload compute (FLOPs): GEMM1 ≈ `2·M·2048·6144`, GEMM2 ≈ `2·M·2048·2048` → **GEMM1 is 3×\n  GEMM2**. The middle (gating+conv+gating) is tiny FLOPs but touches `~5·M·H` bf16 elements → memory-bound.\n- rtol 5% is loose; atol is the tighter constraint (0.0098 on WL5, 0.013 on WL1/2, 0.021 on WL3/4).\n  WL5 (B=1, S=2048, atol 0.0098) is the least forgiving.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Constraints & environment realities\n\n- **Triton-only compute.** Both GEMMs must be Triton `tl.dot` kernels (cannot call `F.linear`/`torch.matmul`\n  for compute — that would be a Torch computational fallback). This is the central performance risk:\n  we must approach cuBLAS bf16 throughput on Ampere with a well-tuned Triton matmul, then win the rest\n  via fusion.\n- **No local execution / no profiler.** Per CLAUDE.md we may not run CUDA, a profiler, `nvidia-smi`,\n  the external evaluator directly, or any alternate correctness harness. The **only** feedback channel\n  is `./scripts/evaluate_candidate.sh feedback cNNN`, and each such run (5 workloads) consumes **one**\n  of 100 evals and reports correctness + speedup. ⇒ correctness must be established **analytically**\n  before spending an eval; a failed Triton run is wasted budget.\n- **Skills:** `KernelWiki` targets Blackwell/Hopper (tcgen05/TMEM/CLC/FP4) and is **not applicable** to\n  `sm_80`; `ncu-report-skill` requires running the profiler, which is disallowed. Both are effectively\n  unusable here beyond generic reading; note in candidate `skill_usage` accordingly.\n- **Immutability:** every meaningful source/config/launch change ⇒ new `cNNN`; never rewrite past records.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Numerical analysis & risks\n\n### 4.1 Reference precision chain\nReference rounds to **bf16 at every stage**: `BCx` (fp32-accum cuBLAS → bf16), `Bx = Bgate*Xproj`\n(bf16 op, internally fp32→round bf16), `conv_out` (cuDNN bf16, fp32-accum → bf16), `y = Cgate*conv_out`\n(→ bf16), then GEMM2 (fp32-accum → bf16). So the \"ground truth\" itself carries bf16 rounding at 4\nintermediate points.\n\n### 4.2 Our precision plan\n- **GEMMs:** load bf16 operands, accumulate in **fp32** (`tl.dot(..., out_dtype=fp32)` / fp32\n  accumulator), add bias in fp32, cast to bf16 on store. Matches cuBLAS behavior.\n- **Middle (gating+conv+gating):** compute entirely in **fp32** (promote loaded bf16 → fp32, multiply,\n  conv-accumulate in fp32, multiply, then cast the final `y` to bf16). This is *more* accurate than the\n  reference's intermediate bf16 roundings, and the differences are well inside the loose tolerances.\n  - Key subtlety: the reference rounds `Bx` to **bf16 before** the conv. If our fp32 path ever drifts\n    outside atol we can insert an explicit `Bx_bf16 = Bx.to(bf16).to(fp32)` round to mirror the\n    reference; keep this as a fallback lever (WL5 with atol 0.0098 is where to watch).\n- **Accumulation order:** conv is only 4 taps → order-insensitive. GEMM2 reduces over `H=2048`; use\n  the standard `BLOCK_K` split with fp32 accumulator (cuBLAS also splits K). Differences here are\n  random-sign and bounded by rtol 0.05 over 2048 terms.\n\n### 4.3 Correctness pitfalls to get right the first time\n1. **Tap order / causal direction:** `k=3` is the current sample; taps go backward in `s`. A mirrored\n   kernel (using `conv_weight[...,K_c-1-k]` or reading `s+k`) will silently pass shape checks but fail\n   numerically. Derive from `F.pad(left=3)` + `conv1d` cross-correlation semantics (PyTorch conv1d is\n   cross-correlation, **not** flipped convolution).\n2. **Batch boundaries:** taps at `s-3+k<0` must be zero; never read row `m-3+k` from the previous batch.\n   Guard with `s - 3 + k ≥ 0` (equivalently `row ≥ b*S`).\n3. **Chunk offsets:** `Bgate=[0,H)`, `Cgate=[H,2H)`, `Xproj=[2H,3H)` — off-by-`H` here is a classic bug.\n4. **`F.linear` transpose:** weights are `(out, in)`; GEMM computes `Σ_in x·W[out,in]` (i.e. `x @ W^T`).\n5. **Bias dtype:** biases are bf16; add in fp32 then round.\n6. **Contiguity of `x`:** reshape `(B,S,H)→(M,H)` is only free if `x` is contiguous (it is per definition);\n   assert strides / use `.reshape` guarded, but do not silently `.contiguous()` an already-contiguous tensor.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Triton design space\n\nBaseline decomposition into **3 Triton kernels** (all in `(M,·)` space, no transposes):\n\n### K1 — GEMM1 with bias: `BCx = x @ in_proj_weight^T + in_proj_bias`, shape `(M, 3H)`\n- Standard tiled bf16 matmul: `BLOCK_M × BLOCK_N` output tile, `BLOCK_K` reduction over `H=2048`,\n  fp32 accumulator, L2-friendly `GROUP_M` row-grouping, `num_warps`/`num_stages` autotuned.\n- `N=6144` and `K=2048` are multiples of 64/128 → no N/K masking; `M∈{1024,2048,8192}` multiples of\n  128 → no M masking for `BLOCK_M∈{64,128,256}`. Clean, mask-free fast path.\n- Candidate tile menu: `BLOCK_M∈{64,128,256}`, `BLOCK_N∈{64,128,256}`, `BLOCK_K∈{32,64,128}`,\n  `num_stages∈{3,4,5}`, `num_warps∈{4,8}`, `GROUP_M∈{4,8}`.\n\n### K2 — fused gating + causal depthwise conv + gating: `y (M, H)`\n- Reads `BCx`, computes `Bx=BCx[:, :H]*BCx[:, 2H:3H]`, the 4-tap causal conv (per-channel weights\n  `conv_weight[h,0,·]`, `conv_bias[h]`), then `y = BCx[:, H:2H] * conv_out`. Pure element-wise + tiny\n  stencil ⇒ **memory-bound**; goal is minimum passes.\n- Tiling options:\n  - (a) **Sequence-tiled**: block `(BLOCK_S rows within one batch) × (BLOCK_H channels)`; load a\n    halo of 3 extra rows above the tile, compute `Bx` once, do the 4-tap stencil in registers/smem.\n    Amortizes the `Bx` computation and the neighbor reads. Grid = `(B, ceil(S/BLOCK_S), ceil(H/BLOCK_H))`.\n  - (b) **Row-parallel** (simple): one program per `(row-block, channel-block)`, recompute the ≤4\n    neighbor `Bx` values by re-loading `Bgate,Xproj` for rows `m-3..m`. Simplest to prove correct;\n    up to 4× redundant loads of `Bgate/Xproj` but conv is cheap. Good **c001** choice.\n  - Batch-boundary guard via `s` index; conv weights broadcast over the row dimension (per-channel).\n- Because `conv_weight`/`conv_bias` are `(H,·)` and reused for all `M` rows, keep them resident per\n  channel-block.\n\n### K3 — GEMM2 with bias: `output = y @ out_proj_weight^T + out_proj_bias`, shape `(M, H)`\n- Same matmul template as K1 with `N=2048`.\n\n### Fusion escalation ladder (later candidates)\n- **F1: fold gating into GEMM1 epilogue.** Produce only `Bx (M,H)` and `Cgate (M,H)` (write `2H`\n  instead of `3H`) by combining the `[0,H)` and `[2H,3H)` column tiles in the epilogue. Needs the\n  epilogue to see both column blocks → either `BLOCK_N` spanning matched columns or a two-pass\n  accumulator; evaluate cost/benefit (saves ~1/3 of the intermediate write+read).\n- **F2: fuse conv into GEMM2's A-load.** GEMM2's `A = y (M,H)`; `y[m,h]` needs `conv_out[m,h]` which\n  needs neighbor rows `m-1..m-3`. Feasible only if `BLOCK_M` rows are contiguous in `M` and we load a\n  3-row halo into the K-loop; complex and risks correctness at batch/tile edges. Treat as a stretch goal\n  only if K2 is shown to be a real bottleneck.\n- **F3: merge K1+K2** so the gating/conv is the GEMM1 epilogue producing `y` directly. Blocked by the\n  conv needing cross-row (cross-`BLOCK_M`) neighbors; likely not worth it. Keep K2 standalone.\n- **GEMM1 restructuring:** single `N=3H` GEMM reuses each `A` tile across all three projections (best A\n  reuse) — prefer over three separate GEMMs.\n\n### Where the time goes (rough Ampere model, M=8192 case)\n- GEMM1 ≈ `2·8192·2048·6144 ≈ 2.06e11` FLOP; GEMM2 ≈ `6.9e10` FLOP; total ≈ `2.75e11`.\n- At ~1.5e14 bf16 FLOP/s realized → ~1.8 ms of unavoidable matmul; K2 memory traffic\n  (`~5·M·H·2 B ≈ 0.17 GB`) at ~1.5 TB/s → ~0.1 ms. **GEMMs dominate.**\n- ⇒ The realistic upside is: (i) our Triton GEMMs must land near cuBLAS, and (ii) we recover the\n  reference's overheads — the slow **cuDNN depthwise `conv1d` with groups=2048**, two transposes, a\n  `.contiguous()` copy, and several extra kernel launches / intermediate allocations. Grouped/depthwise\n  conv over 2048 groups is a known cuDNN weak spot; that plus the transposes is our headroom.\n- **Speedup expectation:** modest, roughly **1.1–1.5×** if GEMMs match cuBLAS; **<1×** risk if the\n  Triton GEMMs underperform cuBLAS by more than the fusion savings. This risk ordering drives the\n  candidate roadmap: first prove correctness + measure the GEMM gap, then tune GEMMs, then tighten fusion.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Validation strategy (given no local execution)\n\n1. **Analytical correctness before any eval.** Re-derive every index map (§1.1, §4.3) and shape on\n   paper; write kernels with `constexpr` H/3H/K_c so shapes are self-documenting. Add cheap host-side\n   `assert`s on dtypes/shapes/contiguity in `run(...)` (metadata only — allowed).\n2. **Start simple (c001 = correctness anchor):** 3 straightforward kernels (K1, K2 row-parallel variant\n   b, K3), conservative single tile config, fp32 middle. The goal of c001 is a **passing, well-defined\n   baseline** and a first speedup number, not peak performance.\n3. **One variable per candidate.** After c001 passes, change exactly one thing per new `cNNN`\n   (autotune GEMM1, then GEMM2, then K2 sequence-tiling, then F1 fusion), so each eval attributes its\n   delta cleanly. Record parent/hash/hypothesis/per-workload result/geomean/decision/eval-count.\n4. **Correctness watch-list:** WL5 (atol 0.0098) is the tightest — if a candidate fails only there,\n   suspect the fp32-vs-bf16 `Bx` rounding (§4.2) or a conv tap/edge bug, not the GEMMs.\n5. **Budget discipline:** 100 evals but token budget is the likely binding constraint; avoid\n   speculative evals. Converge when geomean gains stall, then write `SEARCH_COMPLETE`. Never run `final`\n   without operator approval.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Open questions to resolve empirically (via feedback evals)\n- How close does a tuned Triton bf16 matmul get to cuBLAS on A800 for these `M,N,K`? (Sets the ceiling.)\n- How large is the reference's `conv1d(groups=2048)`+transpose+contiguous overhead as a fraction of\n  total? (Sets our headroom.) Inferred only from the end-to-end speedup deltas across candidates.\n- Is K2 better as sequence-tiled (halo reuse) or row-parallel (simple)? Decide after c001’s number.\n- Does F1 (gating-in-epilogue) pay for its added complexity, or is the standalone memory-bound K2\n  already negligible vs the GEMMs?\n\n## 8. Immediate next step\nWrite `docs/plan.md` with the executable candidate roadmap (c001 correctness anchor → GEMM autotune →\nK2 tiling → fusion escalation), then implement `c001`.\n","numLines":240,"startLine":1,"totalLines":240}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RmUx79hVbR5Zn8GwCywhqk","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Executable Optimization Plan — L1/005 `conv_gated_projection_with_causal_conv`\n2\t\n3\tCompanion to `docs/draft.md` (read that first for the full index-map derivation). This file is the\n4\t**executable roadmap**: how the source is structured, the exact sequential candidate lineage, the\n5\tcorrectness gates each candidate must pass *before* an eval, the per-candidate performance hypothesis,\n6\tthe stopping rule, and the evidence record format. **No code is written or evaluated in this turn.**\n7\t\n8\tTarget: NVIDIA **A800 (`sm_80`, Ampere)**. Compute **must be Triton** (`tl.dot` GEMMs + Triton\n9\telement/stencil kernels); PyTorch only for metadata/launch plumbing. **No** Torch/CPU/NumPy/CUDA-ext\n10\tcomputational fallback — a failed Triton kernel is an invalid candidate, not a fallback opportunity.\n11\tMetric: **geometric-mean speedup vs reference over the 5 fixed feedback workloads, every selected\n12\tworkload must pass correctness**. Budget: 100 evals; tokens soft 1.0M / normal 1.5M / hard 1.65M.\n13\t\n14\tConstants baked as `tl.constexpr`: `H=2048`, `3H=6144`, `K_c=4`, `groups=H` (depthwise).\n15\t\n16\t---\n17\t\n18\t## 0. Ground rules that shape the whole plan\n19\t\n20\t- **The only feedback channel is `./scripts/evaluate_candidate.sh feedback cNNN`**, which runs all 5\n21\t  workloads = **one** eval of the 100 budget and returns correctness + per-workload speedup. We may\n22\t  **not** run CUDA/profiler/`nvidia-smi`/the evaluator directly, or any alternate correctness harness.\n23\t  ⇒ **Correctness is established analytically before every eval.** An eval is spent only when the\n24\t  candidate is believed-correct and represents a *deliberate* single-variable change.\n25\t- **One variable per candidate.** Each `cNNN` changes exactly one meaningful thing vs its parent so the\n26\t  measured delta is attributable. Any meaningful source/config/launch change ⇒ new ID; IDs are\n27\t  immutable and records are append-only in `candidates.jsonl`.\n28\t- **Skills:** `KernelWiki` is Blackwell/Hopper-specific (tcgen05/TMEM/CLC/FP4/2-SM) and does **not**\n29\t  apply to `sm_80`; profiler-based skills are disallowed (can't run `ncu`). Both are unusable for\n30\t  compute decisions here; each candidate's `skill_usage` will record `\"none (KernelWiki N/A for sm_80;\n31\t  profiler disallowed)\"` unless a specific generic note applies.\n32\t\n33\t---\n34\t\n35\t## 1. Solution architecture (`solution/solution.py`)\n36\t\n37\tSingle file exposing `run(x, in_proj_weight, in_proj_bias, conv_weight, conv_bias, out_proj_weight,\n38\tout_proj_bias) -> output`. Structure held constant across candidates (only kernel internals / configs /\n39\tfusion boundaries change per candidate):\n40\t\n41\t```\n42\trun():\n43\t  # --- metadata & plumbing only (PyTorch allowed) ---\n44\t  B, S, H = x.shape ; M = B*S ; assert H==2048, kc==4, 3H==6144\n45\t  assert dtypes are bf16; ensure x contiguous view -> Xv = x.reshape(M, H)  (free view)\n46\t  allocate outputs as bf16 empty tensors\n47\t  # --- Triton compute ---\n48\t  K1: BCx (M, 3H)          = GEMM1(Xv, in_proj_weight, in_proj_bias)          # tl.dot, fp32 accum\n49\t  K2: y   (M, H)           = gating+causal-depthwise-conv+gating(BCx, conv_weight, conv_bias)\n50\t  K3: out (M, H)           = GEMM2(y, out_proj_weight, out_proj_bias)         # tl.dot, fp32 accum\n51\t  return out.reshape(B, S, H)\n52\t```\n53\t\n54\tEverything lives in row-major `(M, ·)` space; the reference's two `transpose`s + `.contiguous()` are\n55\t**eliminated** (they were only artifacts of routing through `conv1d`'s `(B,C,S)` convention). See draft\n56\t§1.1 for why the conv along `M`-rows with an `s = m mod S ≥ k-shift` guard is equivalent.\n57\t\n58\t**Kernel definitions (baseline decomposition):**\n59\t\n60\t- **K1 — GEMM1+bias** → `BCx = Xv @ in_proj_weight^T + in_proj_bias`, `(M, 3H)`. Tiled bf16 matmul,\n61\t  fp32 accumulator, bias added in fp32, cast bf16 on store. `N=6144`, `K=2048` are mult. of 128;\n62\t  `M∈{1024,2048,8192}` mult. of 256 ⇒ **mask-free fast path** for `BLOCK_{M,N,K}∈{64,128,256}`.\n63\t- **K2 — fused gating+conv+gating** → `y`, `(M, H)`. For each output element:\n64\t  `Bx[m,h] = BCx[m,h] * BCx[m,2H+h]` (fp32),\n65\t  `conv_out[m,h] = conv_bias[h] + Σ_{k=0..3} conv_weight[h,0,k] * Bx[b,h, s-3+k]` with the tap masked\n66\t  to 0 when `s-3+k < 0` (causal left-pad = batch-boundary guard, they coincide),\n67\t  `y[m,h] = BCx[m,H+h] * conv_out[m,h]` → cast bf16. Memory-bound.\n68\t- **K3 — GEMM2+bias** → `out = y @ out_proj_weight^T + out_proj_bias`, `(M, H)`. Same template as K1,\n69\t  `N=2048`.\n70\t\n71\t---\n72\t\n73\t## 2. Correctness checks (run BEFORE spending any eval)\n74\t\n75\tThese are the gates every candidate must clear analytically. They are re-verified whenever a candidate\n76\ttouches the relevant code path.\n77\t\n78\t### 2.1 Static/host checks (metadata only — allowed, not compute)\n79\t- Assert `x.shape == (B,S,H)`, `H==2048`, `conv_weight.shape==(H,1,4)`, `in_proj_weight.shape==(6144,2048)`,\n80\t  `out_proj_weight.shape==(2048,2048)`, all inputs `bfloat16`.\n81\t- Assert `x` contiguous so `reshape(M,H)` is a free view (do **not** silently `.contiguous()` an\n82\t  already-contiguous tensor; if ever non-contiguous, `.reshape` will copy — acceptable, note it).\n83\t- Output allocated `(B,S,H)` bf16, returned via `reshape` from `(M,H)`.\n84\t\n85\t### 2.2 Kernel-derivation checklist (from draft §1.1 / §4.3) — the 6 classic bugs\n86\t1. **Chunk offsets:** `Bgate=[0,H)`, `Cgate=[H,2H)`, `Xproj=[2H,3H)`. Gating uses `Bx=BCx[:,0:H]*BCx[:,2H:3H]`;\n87\t   output gate uses `BCx[:,H:2H]`. Off-by-`H` is the most likely silent bug.\n88\t2. **Conv tap order / direction:** PyTorch `conv1d` is **cross-correlation** (not flipped). With\n89\t   `F.pad(left=3)`, output `s` reads `Bx` at `s-3+k` for `k=0..3`; `k=3`=current sample `s` (weight\n90\t   `conv_weight[h,0,3]`), `k=0`=oldest `s-3` (weight `[h,0,0]`). A mirrored kernel passes shapes, fails\n91\t   numerics.\n92\t3. **Causal / batch-boundary mask:** tap valid iff `s-3+k ≥ 0` (equivalently global row `≥ b*S`). Never\n93\t   read a neighbor row from the previous batch.\n94\t4. **`F.linear` transpose:** weights `(out,in)`; compute `Σ_in x[m,in]·W[out,in]` = `x @ W^T`.\n95\t5. **Bias dtype:** biases are bf16; promote to fp32, add to fp32 accumulator, then round to bf16.\n96\t6. **Accumulation precision:** GEMMs accumulate fp32 (matches cuBLAS); conv accumulates fp32 (4 taps,\n97\t   order-insensitive); middle computed fully in fp32.\n98\t\n99\t### 2.3 Numerical-tolerance reasoning (why fp32 middle is safe)\n100\tReference rounds to bf16 at 4 intermediate points (`BCx`, `Bx`, `conv_out`, `y`); our fp32 middle is\n101\t*more* accurate. Tolerances: atol WL5 0.0098 (tightest), WL1/2 0.013, WL3/4 0.021; rtol 0.05 (loose).\n102\t**WL5 (B=1,S=2048) is the correctness watch-list case.** Fallback lever if WL5 fails only numerically:\n103\tinsert an explicit `Bx = Bx.to(bf16).to(fp32)` round before the conv to mirror the reference's\n104\tpre-conv bf16 rounding. This lever is a *new candidate* (source change), not an in-place patch.\n105\t\n106\t### 2.4 Failure triage table (map a feedback failure → suspected cause, without a profiler)\n107\t| Symptom | Prime suspects | Action |\n108\t|---|---|---|\n109\t| All 5 wrong by large margin | chunk offset (#1) or `W` vs `W^T` (#4) | re-derive index map, fix, new candidate |\n110\t| All wrong by small margin near atol | conv tap/edge (#2/#3) or bias dtype (#5) | check tap order + boundary guard |\n111\t| Only WL5 fails, others pass | fp32-vs-bf16 `Bx` rounding (§2.3) | apply the bf16-round lever candidate |\n112\t| Only large-M (WL3/4) fails | M-tiling / mask / accumulator overflow | check BLOCK_M bounds, no ragged mask needed |\n113\t| Correct but slow (<1×) | Triton GEMM below cuBLAS | GEMM autotune candidates (§3) |\n114\t\n115\t---\n116\t\n117\t## 3. Candidate lineage strategy (sequential roadmap)\n118\t\n119\tEach step lists **parent → hypothesis → single change → expected metric effect → decision rule**.\n120\tLater steps are contingent on earlier results; the exact continuation is chosen from feedback, but the\n121\tpriority order is fixed by the draft's roofline (GEMMs dominate: GEMM1 ≈ 3× GEMM2 FLOPs; middle is\n122\tmemory-bound and tiny — draft §5 \"where the time goes\").\n123\t\n124\t### Phase A — Correctness anchor\n125\t- **c001 — baseline, prove correctness.** Parent: none. 3 kernels (K1, K2 *row-parallel* simple\n126\t  variant, K3), **single conservative tile config** (e.g. `BLOCK_M=64, BLOCK_N=64, BLOCK_K=32,\n127\t  num_warps=4, num_stages=3`), fp32 middle. Goal: **pass all 5 workloads** and get a first speedup\n128\t  number + the GEMM-gap read. Decision: if any workload fails → triage via §2.4, fix as c002 (still\n129\t  \"anchor\"), do **not** proceed to tuning until correctness holds. If passes → record baseline geomean.\n130\t\n131\t### Phase B — GEMM tuning (highest leverage; GEMMs dominate)\n132\t- **c00x — autotune K1 (GEMM1).** Parent: last-correct anchor. Single change: add `@triton.autotune`\n133\t  over the tile menu (draft §5: `BLOCK_M∈{64,128,256}`, `BLOCK_N∈{64,128,256}`, `BLOCK_K∈{32,64,128}`,\n134\t  `num_stages∈{3,4,5}`, `num_warps∈{4,8}`, `GROUP_M∈{4,8}`) for K1 only. K2/K3 unchanged. Hypothesis:\n135\t  GEMM1 is the largest single cost (N=6144); best tile picks up most of the win. Expect the biggest\n136\t  single jump here. Decision: keep if geomean improves and correctness holds.\n137\t- **c00x — autotune K3 (GEMM2).** Parent: previous. Single change: same autotune menu on K3 (N=2048).\n138\t  Hypothesis: second-largest GEMM. Expect a smaller-but-real gain. Keep on improvement.\n139\t- **c00x — L2 grouping / persistent scheduling tweak on GEMMs (optional).** Only if the GEMM gap to the\n140\t  expected cuBLAS ceiling still looks large. Try `GROUP_M` sweep / split-K for the small-M cases\n141\t  (M=1024). Keep on improvement; else revert (record as rejected).\n142\t\n143\t### Phase C — Fusion of the memory-bound middle\n144\t- **c00x — K2 sequence-tiling with halo reuse.** Parent: best GEMM candidate. Single change: replace K2\n145\t  row-parallel variant with sequence-tiled `(BLOCK_S within one batch × BLOCK_H)` that loads a 3-row\n146\t  halo and computes `Bx` once (draft §5 K2 option a). Hypothesis: removes up to 4× redundant\n147\t  `Bgate/Xproj` loads; but K2 is memory-bound and small vs GEMMs, so effect is small. Keep only if\n148\t  measurable and correctness holds.\n149\t- **c00x — F1: fold gating into GEMM1 epilogue.** Parent: best so far. Single change: K1 writes only\n150\t  `Bx (M,H)` and `Cgate (M,H)` (2H columns) by combining the `[0,H)`×`[2H,3H)` epilogue, so K2 no\n151\t  longer recomputes the input gate. Hypothesis: saves ~1/3 of the intermediate write+read. Risk: needs\n152\t  epilogue to see both column blocks (N tiling or 2-pass accumulator). Keep on improvement.\n153\t\n154\t### Phase D — Stretch fusions (only if justified by measured bottleneck)\n155\t- **c00x — F2: fuse conv into GEMM2 A-load** (draft §5 F2). High complexity/correctness risk at\n156\t  batch/tile edges; attempt only if K2+intermediate traffic is shown to matter after Phase C.\n157\t- **c00x — merge K1+K2** (F3) — likely not worth it (conv needs cross-`BLOCK_M` neighbors); keep as a\n158\t  last idea only.\n159\t\n160\t**Numbering:** candidates are numbered strictly sequentially (`c001`, `c002`, …) in creation order;\n161\tthe phase labels above are logical, not literal IDs. If an early candidate fails correctness, the fix\n162\tconsumes the next ID (it is a new source version) — never reuse an ID.\n163\t\n164\t---\n165\t\n166\t## 4. Performance hypotheses (roofline expectations)\n167\t\n168\tFrom draft §5 (Ampere model, M=8192): GEMM1 ≈ 2.06e11 FLOP, GEMM2 ≈ 6.9e10 FLOP (GEMM1 ≈ 3× GEMM2);\n169\tmiddle traffic ≈ 0.17 GB. **GEMMs dominate; the middle is a rounding error on the timeline.**\n170\t- **Ceiling:** set by how close a tuned Triton bf16 matmul gets to cuBLAS on A800 for these M,N,K.\n171\t- **Headroom (our win source):** recovering the reference's overheads — slow cuDNN depthwise\n172\t  `conv1d(groups=2048)`, two transposes, a `.contiguous()` copy, and extra kernel launches/allocs.\n173\t- **Overall expectation:** modest **≈1.1–1.5×** if Triton GEMMs match cuBLAS; **<1× risk** if they\n174\t  underperform cuBLAS by more than the fusion savings. This risk ordering is exactly why Phase B (GEMM\n175\t  tuning) precedes Phase C (fusion): first close the GEMM gap, then harvest fusion.\n176\t- **Falsifiable predictions:** (P1) c001→GEMM1-autotune yields the single largest jump; (P2) K2\n177\t  sequence-tiling and F1 each yield small gains (middle is memory-bound & minor); (P3) if even the best\n178\t  GEMM candidate is <1×, the Triton matmul cannot beat cuBLAS here and the task's realistic ceiling is\n179\t  near the fusion savings alone — converge early rather than burn evals.\n180\t\n181\t---\n182\t\n183\t## 5. Stopping criteria\n184\t\n185\tStop and write `SEARCH_COMPLETE` (with the reason) when **any** of:\n186\t1. **Convergence:** best geomean improves by **< ~1%** across **2 consecutive** accepted candidates, and\n187\t   the remaining roadmap items are the low-yield ones (Phase C/D) whose predicted gains are within noise.\n188\t2. **Budget:** approaching the token soft limit (1.0M) with no candidate in flight expected to move the\n189\t   metric materially, or the 100-eval cap is near. Token budget is expected to bind before 100 evals.\n190\t3. **Ceiling hit:** GEMM tuning has plateaued (Phase B exhausted), fusion (Phase C) added, and further\n191\t   ideas are Phase-D stretch fusions judged not worth the correctness risk for the roofline headroom.\n192\t4. **No viable win:** if the best *correct* candidate is persistently <1× and analysis (P3) shows the\n193\t   Triton-vs-cuBLAS gap exceeds all fusion savings, stop and report the best correct candidate.\n194\t\n195\tRecord the chosen best **valid** (all-workloads-correct) candidate as the final selection. **Never run\n196\t`./scripts/evaluate_candidate.sh final <id>` without explicit operator approval.**\n197\t\n198\t---\n199\t\n200\t## 6. Evidence format (append one JSON object per eval to `candidates.jsonl`)\n201\t\n202\tAppend-only; never rewrite earlier records. One object per **evaluated** candidate (an eval = all 5\n203\tfeedback workloads). Schema:\n204\t\n205\t```json\n206\t{\n207\t  \"candidate_id\": \"c001\",\n208\t  \"parent\": null,\n209\t  \"source_sha256\": \"<hash of solution/solution.py at eval time>\",\n210\t  \"hypothesis\": \"Correctness anchor: 3-kernel decomposition, fp32 middle, single conservative tile.\",\n211\t  \"change_vs_parent\": \"initial baseline\",\n212\t  \"validation\": {\n213\t    \"analytical_checks\": [\"chunk offsets\", \"conv tap/dir\", \"causal+batch mask\", \"W^T\", \"fp32 accum\"],\n214\t    \"expected_correct\": true\n215\t  },\n216\t  \"results\": {\n217\t    \"b0a9e3f0\": {\"axes\": {\"B\":1,\"S\":1024},  \"correct\": null, \"speedup\": null},\n218\t    \"8b678b13\": {\"axes\": {\"B\":4,\"S\":256},   \"correct\": null, \"speedup\": null},\n219\t    \"58e3ac47\": {\"axes\": {\"B\":32,\"S\":256},  \"correct\": null, \"speedup\": null},\n220\t    \"6033fd0f\": {\"axes\": {\"B\":2,\"S\":4096},  \"correct\": null, \"speedup\": null},\n221\t    \"bb262a64\": {\"axes\": {\"B\":1,\"S\":2048},  \"correct\": null, \"speedup\": null}\n222\t  },\n223\t  \"geomean_speedup\": null,\n224\t  \"all_correct\": null,\n225\t  \"decision\": \"accept | reject | anchor-fix\",\n226\t  \"decision_reason\": \"<why kept/dropped; which prediction confirmed/refuted>\",\n227\t  \"cumulative_evals\": 1,\n228\t  \"skill_usage\": \"none (KernelWiki N/A for sm_80; profiler disallowed)\"\n229\t}\n230\t```\n231\t\n232\tField rules:\n233\t- `speedup` values are copied verbatim from the launcher's per-workload report; `geomean_speedup` is the\n234\t  geometric mean over the 5 workloads (only meaningful if `all_correct` is true).\n235\t- `decision`: **accept** if `all_correct && geomean improved over current best`; **reject** otherwise\n236\t  (kept in the log for lineage); **anchor-fix** for a correctness repair of a failing anchor.\n237\t- `parent` = the candidate this one was derived from (the current best-correct, unless triaging a bug).\n238\t- `cumulative_evals` = running count of feedback evals consumed (out of 100).\n239\t- `source_sha256` recomputed from the exact `solution/solution.py` submitted for that ID (immutability\n240\t  proof; a changed source always gets a new ID).\n241\t\n242\t---\n243\t\n244\t## 7. Execution checklist (per candidate, mechanical)\n245\t\n246\t1. Write/modify `solution/solution.py` for the **single** intended change; keep the `run()` contract.\n247\t2. Re-run the §2 correctness checklist for every touched path; do not eval unless believed-correct.\n248\t3. Freeze the source as the immutable candidate `cNNN` (record `source_sha256`).\n249\t4. `./scripts/evaluate_candidate.sh feedback cNNN` (one eval).\n250\t5. Append the §6 record to `candidates.jsonl` (never edit prior lines).\n251\t6. Update \"current best-correct\" pointer; pick next candidate per §3 priority using §4 predictions.\n252\t7. Re-check §5 stopping criteria; if met, write `SEARCH_COMPLETE` with the reason and stop.\n253\t\n254\t---\n255\t\n256\t## 8. Immediate next step (next turn, not now)\n257\tImplement **c001** (the correctness anchor): 3-kernel Triton decomposition in `solution/solution.py`,\n258\tfp32 middle, single conservative GEMM config, with the §2 host asserts. Then run one feedback eval and\n259\trecord it. Do not tune before c001 is correct.\n260\t\n261\t---\n262\t\n263\t## 9. Decision log\n264\t\n265\t### c001 — DONE (eval 1/100). Accepted as current best-correct.\n266\t- Result: **all 5 correct**, geomean **1.0809×**. Per-workload: WL1 1.167, WL2 1.171,\n267\t  **WL3 0.889** (M=8192, B32/S256), WL4 1.026 (M=8192, B2/S4096), WL5 1.184.\n268\t- Reading: correctness derivation validated end-to-end (no numerical failure, even WL5 atol 0.0098 —\n269\t  so the fp32 middle is safe; the §2.3 bf16-round lever is **not** needed). Prediction **P1 confirmed**:\n270\t  the GEMMs dominate and the conservative `64×64×32` tile is the bottleneck at large M. WL3 is <1×\n271\t  (cuBLAS beats our untuned tile); WL4 (same M=8192 but S=4096) is 1.026×, so the deficit is tile-shape\n272\t  driven, not batch-boundary driven.\n273\t- **Next candidate → c002 (Phase B): autotune the GEMM kernel** (`_gemm_bias_kernel`, used by both K1\n274\t  and K3) over the draft §5 tile menu (`BLOCK_M∈{64,128,256}`, `BLOCK_N∈{64,128,256}`,\n275\t  `BLOCK_K∈{32,64,128}`, `num_stages∈{3,4,5}`, `num_warps∈{4,8}`, `GROUP_M∈{4,8}`), keying on\n276\t  `(M,N,K)`. Single change vs c001: add `@triton.autotune`; K2 untouched. Expected: recover WL3 to ≥1×\n277\t  and lift the large-M cases, biggest single geomean jump. Note: autotuning both GEMMs at once is the\n278\t  minimal source change (they share one kernel); this is still \"one variable\" (the GEMM tile policy).\n279\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b8e044ef-369c-4078-b4f1-f9181e49de1a","uuid":"1b4f9d22-1d31-4471-9c78-25f4d77f09d8","timestamp":"2026-09-16T17:25:35.922Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/data1/workspace/weihongren/kda-runs/formal-kda-20260916--sol_execbench--L1-005_conv_gated_projection_with_causal_conv/docs/plan.md","content":"# Executable Optimization Plan — L1/005 `conv_gated_projection_with_causal_conv`\n\nCompanion to `docs/draft.md` (read that first for the full index-map derivation). This file is the\n**executable roadmap**: how the source is structured, the exact sequential candidate lineage, the\ncorrectness gates each candidate must pass *before* an eval, the per-candidate performance hypothesis,\nthe stopping rule, and the evidence record format. **No code is written or evaluated in this turn.**\n\nTarget: NVIDIA **A800 (`sm_80`, Ampere)**. Compute **must be Triton** (`tl.dot` GEMMs + Triton\nelement/stencil kernels); PyTorch only for metadata/launch plumbing. **No** Torch/CPU/NumPy/CUDA-ext\ncomputational fallback — a failed Triton kernel is an invalid candidate, not a fallback opportunity.\nMetric: **geometric-mean speedup vs reference over the 5 fixed feedback workloads, every selected\nworkload must pass correctness**. Budget: 100 evals; tokens soft 1.0M / normal 1.5M / hard 1.65M.\n\nConstants baked as `tl.constexpr`: `H=2048`, `3H=6144`, `K_c=4`, `groups=H` (depthwise).\n\n---\n\n## 0. Ground rules that shape the whole plan\n\n- **The only feedback channel is `./scripts/evaluate_candidate.sh feedback cNNN`**, which runs all 5\n  workloads = **one** eval of the 100 budget and returns correctness + per-workload speedup. We may\n  **not** run CUDA/profiler/`nvidia-smi`/the evaluator directly, or any alternate correctness harness.\n  ⇒ **Correctness is established analytically before every eval.** An eval is spent only when the\n  candidate is believed-correct and represents a *deliberate* single-variable change.\n- **One variable per candidate.** Each `cNNN` changes exactly one meaningful thing vs its parent so the\n  measured delta is attributable. Any meaningful source/config/launch change ⇒ new ID; IDs are\n  immutable and records are append-only in `candidates.jsonl`.\n- **Skills:** `KernelWiki` is Blackwell/Hopper-specific (tcgen05/TMEM/CLC/FP4/2-SM) and does **not**\n  apply to `sm_80`; profiler-based skills are disallowed (can't run `ncu`). Both are unusable for\n  compute decisions here; each candidate's `skill_usage` will record `\"none (KernelWiki N/A for sm_80;\n  profiler disallowed)\"` unless a specific generic note applies.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Solution architecture (`solution/solution.py`)\n\nSingle file exposing `run(x, in_proj_weight, in_proj_bias, conv_weight, conv_bias, out_proj_weight,\nout_proj_bias) -> output`. Structure held constant across candidates (only kernel internals / configs /\nfusion boundaries change per candidate):\n\n```\nrun():\n  # --- metadata & plumbing only (PyTorch allowed) ---\n  B, S, H = x.shape ; M = B*S ; assert H==2048, kc==4, 3H==6144\n  assert dtypes are bf16; ensure x contiguous view -> Xv = x.reshape(M, H)  (free view)\n  allocate outputs as bf16 empty tensors\n  # --- Triton compute ---\n  K1: BCx (M, 3H)          = GEMM1(Xv, in_proj_weight, in_proj_bias)          # tl.dot, fp32 accum\n  K2: y   (M, H)           = gating+causal-depthwise-conv+gating(BCx, conv_weight, conv_bias)\n  K3: out (M, H)           = GEMM2(y, out_proj_weight, out_proj_bias)         # tl.dot, fp32 accum\n  return out.reshape(B, S, H)\n```\n\nEverything lives in row-major `(M, ·)` space; the reference's two `transpose`s + `.contiguous()` are\n**eliminated** (they were only artifacts of routing through `conv1d`'s `(B,C,S)` convention). See draft\n§1.1 for why the conv along `M`-rows with an `s = m mod S ≥ k-shift` guard is equivalent.\n\n**Kernel definitions (baseline decomposition):**\n\n- **K1 — GEMM1+bias** → `BCx = Xv @ in_proj_weight^T + in_proj_bias`, `(M, 3H)`. Tiled bf16 matmul,\n  fp32 accumulator, bias added in fp32, cast bf16 on store. `N=6144`, `K=2048` are mult. of 128;\n  `M∈{1024,2048,8192}` mult. of 256 ⇒ **mask-free fast path** for `BLOCK_{M,N,K}∈{64,128,256}`.\n- **K2 — fused gating+conv+gating** → `y`, `(M, H)`. For each output element:\n  `Bx[m,h] = BCx[m,h] * BCx[m,2H+h]` (fp32),\n  `conv_out[m,h] = conv_bias[h] + Σ_{k=0..3} conv_weight[h,0,k] * Bx[b,h, s-3+k]` with the tap masked\n  to 0 when `s-3+k < 0` (causal left-pad = batch-boundary guard, they coincide),\n  `y[m,h] = BCx[m,H+h] * conv_out[m,h]` → cast bf16. Memory-bound.\n- **K3 — GEMM2+bias** → `out = y @ out_proj_weight^T + out_proj_bias`, `(M, H)`. Same template as K1,\n  `N=2048`.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Correctness checks (run BEFORE spending any eval)\n\nThese are the gates every candidate must clear analytically. They are re-verified whenever a candidate\ntouches the relevant code path.\n\n### 2.1 Static/host checks (metadata only — allowed, not compute)\n- Assert `x.shape == (B,S,H)`, `H==2048`, `conv_weight.shape==(H,1,4)`, `in_proj_weight.shape==(6144,2048)`,\n  `out_proj_weight.shape==(2048,2048)`, all inputs `bfloat16`.\n- Assert `x` contiguous so `reshape(M,H)` is a free view (do **not** silently `.contiguous()` an\n  already-contiguous tensor; if ever non-contiguous, `.reshape` will copy — acceptable, note it).\n- Output allocated `(B,S,H)` bf16, returned via `reshape` from `(M,H)`.\n\n### 2.2 Kernel-derivation checklist (from draft §1.1 / §4.3) — the 6 classic bugs\n1. **Chunk offsets:** `Bgate=[0,H)`, `Cgate=[H,2H)`, `Xproj=[2H,3H)`. Gating uses `Bx=BCx[:,0:H]*BCx[:,2H:3H]`;\n   output gate uses `BCx[:,H:2H]`. Off-by-`H` is the most likely silent bug.\n2. **Conv tap order / direction:** PyTorch `conv1d` is **cross-correlation** (not flipped). With\n   `F.pad(left=3)`, output `s` reads `Bx` at `s-3+k` for `k=0..3`; `k=3`=current sample `s` (weight\n   `conv_weight[h,0,3]`), `k=0`=oldest `s-3` (weight `[h,0,0]`). A mirrored kernel passes shapes, fails\n   numerics.\n3. **Causal / batch-boundary mask:** tap valid iff `s-3+k ≥ 0` (equivalently global row `≥ b*S`). Never\n   read a neighbor row from the previous batch.\n4. **`F.linear` transpose:** weights `(out,in)`; compute `Σ_in x[m,in]·W[out,in]` = `x @ W^T`.\n5. **Bias dtype:** biases are bf16; promote to fp32, add to fp32 accumulator, then round to bf16.\n6. **Accumulation precision:** GEMMs accumulate fp32 (matches cuBLAS); conv accumulates fp32 (4 taps,\n   order-insensitive); middle computed fully in fp32.\n\n### 2.3 Numerical-tolerance reasoning (why fp32 middle is safe)\nReference rounds to bf16 at 4 intermediate points (`BCx`, `Bx`, `conv_out`, `y`); our fp32 middle is\n*more* accurate. Tolerances: atol WL5 0.0098 (tightest), WL1/2 0.013, WL3/4 0.021; rtol 0.05 (loose).\n**WL5 (B=1,S=2048) is the correctness watch-list case.** Fallback lever if WL5 fails only numerically:\ninsert an explicit `Bx = Bx.to(bf16).to(fp32)` round before the conv to mirror the reference's\npre-conv bf16 rounding. This lever is a *new candidate* (source change), not an in-place patch.\n\n### 2.4 Failure triage table (map a feedback failure → suspected cause, without a profiler)\n| Symptom | Prime suspects | Action |\n|---|---|---|\n| All 5 wrong by large margin | chunk offset (#1) or `W` vs `W^T` (#4) | re-derive index map, fix, new candidate |\n| All wrong by small margin near atol | conv tap/edge (#2/#3) or bias dtype (#5) | check tap order + boundary guard |\n| Only WL5 fails, others pass | fp32-vs-bf16 `Bx` rounding (§2.3) | apply the bf16-round lever candidate |\n| Only large-M (WL3/4) fails | M-tiling / mask / accumulator overflow | check BLOCK_M bounds, no ragged mask needed |\n| Correct but slow (<1×) | Triton GEMM below cuBLAS | GEMM autotune candidates (§3) |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Candidate lineage strategy (sequential roadmap)\n\nEach step lists **parent → hypothesis → single change → expected metric effect → decision rule**.\nLater steps are contingent on earlier results; the exact continuation is chosen from feedback, but the\npriority order is fixed by the draft's roofline (GEMMs dominate: GEMM1 ≈ 3× GEMM2 FLOPs; middle is\nmemory-bound and tiny — draft §5 \"where the time goes\").\n\n### Phase A — Correctness anchor\n- **c001 — baseline, prove correctness.** Parent: none. 3 kernels (K1, K2 *row-parallel* simple\n  variant, K3), **single conservative tile config** (e.g. `BLOCK_M=64, BLOCK_N=64, BLOCK_K=32,\n  num_warps=4, num_stages=3`), fp32 middle. Goal: **pass all 5 workloads** and get a first speedup\n  number + the GEMM-gap read. Decision: if any workload fails → triage via §2.4, fix as c002 (still\n  \"anchor\"), do **not** proceed to tuning until correctness holds. If passes → record baseline geomean.\n\n### Phase B — GEMM tuning (highest leverage; GEMMs dominate)\n- **c00x — autotune K1 (GEMM1).** Parent: last-correct anchor. Single change: add `@triton.autotune`\n  over the tile menu (draft §5: `BLOCK_M∈{64,128,256}`, `BLOCK_N∈{64,128,256}`, `BLOCK_K∈{32,64,128}`,\n  `num_stages∈{3,4,5}`, `num_warps∈{4,8}`, `GROUP_M∈{4,8}`) for K1 only. K2/K3 unchanged. Hypothesis:\n  GEMM1 is the largest single cost (N=6144); best tile picks up most of the win. Expect the biggest\n  single jump here. Decision: keep if geomean improves and correctness holds.\n- **c00x — autotune K3 (GEMM2).** Parent: previous. Single change: same autotune menu on K3 (N=2048).\n  Hypothesis: second-largest GEMM. Expect a smaller-but-real gain. Keep on improvement.\n- **c00x — L2 grouping / persistent scheduling tweak on GEMMs (optional).** Only if the GEMM gap to the\n  expected cuBLAS ceiling still looks large. Try `GROUP_M` sweep / split-K for the small-M cases\n  (M=1024). Keep on improvement; else revert (record as rejected).\n\n### Phase C — Fusion of the memory-bound middle\n- **c00x — K2 sequence-tiling with halo reuse.** Parent: best GEMM candidate. Single change: replace K2\n  row-parallel variant with sequence-tiled `(BLOCK_S within one batch × BLOCK_H)` that loads a 3-row\n  halo and computes `Bx` once (draft §5 K2 option a). Hypothesis: removes up to 4× redundant\n  `Bgate/Xproj` loads; but K2 is memory-bound and small vs GEMMs, so effect is small. Keep only if\n  measurable and correctness holds.\n- **c00x — F1: fold gating into GEMM1 epilogue.** Parent: best so far. Single change: K1 writes only\n  `Bx (M,H)` and `Cgate (M,H)` (2H columns) by combining the `[0,H)`×`[2H,3H)` epilogue, so K2 no\n  longer recomputes the input gate. Hypothesis: saves ~1/3 of the intermediate write+read. Risk: needs\n  epilogue to see both column blocks (N tiling or 2-pass accumulator). Keep on improvement.\n\n### Phase D — Stretch fusions (only if justified by measured bottleneck)\n- **c00x — F2: fuse conv into GEMM2 A-load** (draft §5 F2). High complexity/correctness risk at\n  batch/tile edges; attempt only if K2+intermediate traffic is shown to matter after Phase C.\n- **c00x — merge K1+K2** (F3) — likely not worth it (conv needs cross-`BLOCK_M` neighbors); keep as a\n  last idea only.\n\n**Numbering:** candidates are numbered strictly sequentially (`c001`, `c002`, …) in creation order;\nthe phase labels above are logical, not literal IDs. If an early candidate fails correctness, the fix\nconsumes the next ID (it is a new source version) — never reuse an ID.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Performance hypotheses (roofline expectations)\n\nFrom draft §5 (Ampere model, M=8192): GEMM1 ≈ 2.06e11 FLOP, GEMM2 ≈ 6.9e10 FLOP (GEMM1 ≈ 3× GEMM2);\nmiddle traffic ≈ 0.17 GB. **GEMMs dominate; the middle is a rounding error on the timeline.**\n- **Ceiling:** set by how close a tuned Triton bf16 matmul gets to cuBLAS on A800 for these M,N,K.\n- **Headroom (our win source):** recovering the reference's overheads — slow cuDNN depthwise\n  `conv1d(groups=2048)`, two transposes, a `.contiguous()` copy, and extra kernel launches/allocs.\n- **Overall expectation:** modest **≈1.1–1.5×** if Triton GEMMs match cuBLAS; **<1× risk** if they\n  underperform cuBLAS by more than the fusion savings. This risk ordering is exactly why Phase B (GEMM\n  tuning) precedes Phase C (fusion): first close the GEMM gap, then harvest fusion.\n- **Falsifiable predictions:** (P1) c001→GEMM1-autotune yields the single largest jump; (P2) K2\n  sequence-tiling and F1 each yield small gains (middle is memory-bound & minor); (P3) if even the best\n  GEMM candidate is <1×, the Triton matmul cannot beat cuBLAS here and the task's realistic ceiling is\n  near the fusion savings alone — converge early rather than burn evals.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Stopping criteria\n\nStop and write `SEARCH_COMPLETE` (with the reason) when **any** of:\n1. **Convergence:** best geomean improves by **< ~1%** across **2 consecutive** accepted candidates, and\n   the remaining roadmap items are the low-yield ones (Phase C/D) whose predicted gains are within noise.\n2. **Budget:** approaching the token soft limit (1.0M) with no candidate in flight expected to move the\n   metric materially, or the 100-eval cap is near. Token budget is expected to bind before 100 evals.\n3. **Ceiling hit:** GEMM tuning has plateaued (Phase B exhausted), fusion (Phase C) added, and further\n   ideas are Phase-D stretch fusions judged not worth the correctness risk for the roofline headroom.\n4. **No viable win:** if the best *correct* candidate is persistently <1× and analysis (P3) shows the\n   Triton-vs-cuBLAS gap exceeds all fusion savings, stop and report the best correct candidate.\n\nRecord the chosen best **valid** (all-workloads-correct) candidate as the final selection. **Never run\n`./scripts/evaluate_candidate.sh final <id>` without explicit operator approval.**\n\n---\n\n## 6. Evidence format (append one JSON object per eval to `candidates.jsonl`)\n\nAppend-only; never rewrite earlier records. One object per **evaluated** candidate (an eval = all 5\nfeedback workloads). Schema:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"candidate_id\": \"c001\",\n  \"parent\": null,\n  \"source_sha256\": \"<hash of solution/solution.py at eval time>\",\n  \"hypothesis\": \"Correctness anchor: 3-kernel decomposition, fp32 middle, single conservative tile.\",\n  \"change_vs_parent\": \"initial baseline\",\n  \"validation\": {\n    \"analytical_checks\": [\"chunk offsets\", \"conv tap/dir\", \"causal+batch mask\", \"W^T\", \"fp32 accum\"],\n    \"expected_correct\": true\n  },\n  \"results\": {\n    \"b0a9e3f0\": {\"axes\": {\"B\":1,\"S\":1024},  \"correct\": null, \"speedup\": null},\n    \"8b678b13\": {\"axes\": {\"B\":4,\"S\":256},   \"correct\": null, \"speedup\": null},\n    \"58e3ac47\": {\"axes\": {\"B\":32,\"S\":256},  \"correct\": null, \"speedup\": null},\n    \"6033fd0f\": {\"axes\": {\"B\":2,\"S\":4096},  \"correct\": null, \"speedup\": null},\n    \"bb262a64\": {\"axes\": {\"B\":1,\"S\":2048},  \"correct\": null, \"speedup\": null}\n  },\n  \"geomean_speedup\": null,\n  \"all_correct\": null,\n  \"decision\": \"accept | reject | anchor-fix\",\n  \"decision_reason\": \"<why kept/dropped; which prediction confirmed/refuted>\",\n  \"cumulative_evals\": 1,\n  \"skill_usage\": \"none (KernelWiki N/A for sm_80; profiler disallowed)\"\n}\n```\n\nField rules:\n- `speedup` values are copied verbatim from the launcher's per-workload report; `geomean_speedup` is the\n  geometric mean over the 5 workloads (only meaningful if `all_correct` is true).\n- `decision`: **accept** if `all_correct && geomean improved over current best`; **reject** otherwise\n  (kept in the log for lineage); **anchor-fix** for a correctness repair of a failing anchor.\n- `parent` = the candidate this one was derived from (the current best-correct, unless triaging a bug).\n- `cumulative_evals` = running count of feedback evals consumed (out of 100).\n- `source_sha256` recomputed from the exact `solution/solution.py` submitted for that ID (immutability\n  proof; a changed source always gets a new ID).\n\n---\n\n## 7. Execution checklist (per candidate, mechanical)\n\n1. Write/modify `solution/solution.py` for the **single** intended change; keep the `run()` contract.\n2. Re-run the §2 correctness checklist for every touched path; do not eval unless believed-correct.\n3. Freeze the source as the immutable candidate `cNNN` (record `source_sha256`).\n4. `./scripts/evaluate_candidate.sh feedback cNNN` (one eval).\n5. Append the §6 record to `candidates.jsonl` (never edit prior lines).\n6. Update \"current best-correct\" pointer; pick next candidate per §3 priority using §4 predictions.\n7. Re-check §5 stopping criteria; if met, write `SEARCH_COMPLETE` with the reason and stop.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Immediate next step (next turn, not now)\nImplement **c001** (the correctness anchor): 3-kernel Triton decomposition in `solution/solution.py`,\nfp32 middle, single conservative GEMM config, with the §2 host asserts. Then run one feedback eval and\nrecord it. Do not tune before c001 is correct.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Decision log\n\n### c001 — DONE (eval 1/100). Accepted as current best-correct.\n- Result: **all 5 correct**, geomean **1.0809×**. Per-workload: WL1 1.167, WL2 1.171,\n  **WL3 0.889** (M=8192, B32/S256), WL4 1.026 (M=8192, B2/S4096), WL5 1.184.\n- Reading: correctness derivation validated end-to-end (no numerical failure, even WL5 atol 0.0098 —\n  so the fp32 middle is safe; the §2.3 bf16-round lever is **not** needed). Prediction **P1 confirmed**:\n  the GEMMs dominate and the conservative `64×64×32` tile is the bottleneck at large M. WL3 is <1×\n  (cuBLAS beats our untuned tile); WL4 (same M=8192 but S=4096) is 1.026×, so the deficit is tile-shape\n  driven, not batch-boundary driven.\n- **Next candidate → c002 (Phase B): autotune the GEMM kernel** (`_gemm_bias_kernel`, used by both K1\n  and K3) over the draft §5 tile menu (`BLOCK_M∈{64,128,256}`, `BLOCK_N∈{64,128,256}`,\n  `BLOCK_K∈{32,64,128}`, `num_stages∈{3,4,5}`, `num_warps∈{4,8}`, `GROUP_M∈{4,8}`), keying on\n  `(M,N,K)`. Single change vs c001: add `@triton.autotune`; K2 untouched. Expected: recover WL3 to ≥1×\n  and lift the large-M cases, biggest single geomean jump. Note: autotuning both GEMMs at once is the\n  minimal source change (they share one kernel); this is still \"one variable\" (the GEMM tile policy).\n","numLines":279,"startLine":1,"totalLines":279}}}
